# AWOW AK41 always-on core — configuration.
#
# Copy this to `.env` (same directory) and fill in the values. The autoinstall
# first-boot flow writes a `.env` from these placeholders (see autoinstall/).
# NOTHING here is committed — .env is gitignored; only THIS .example is tracked.
# Every value below is a PLACEHOLDER — fill with your own; never commit a real one.
#
# QUOTING RULE: this file is read BOTH by docker compose AND shell-sourced by
# firstboot.sh / provision-technitium.sh — any value containing a SPACE must be
# double-quoted (`KEY="a b"`), or first boot dies at the source line (found
# 2026-07-10; compose strips the quotes, so quoting is safe for both readers).

# ── Domains & network ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Public apex domain your router forwards to this box.
DOMAIN=example.tld
# LAN IP of THIS AWOW box. Split-horizon records point the subdomains here so
# home devices connect directly and TLS still validates.
# Give the box a DHCP reservation / static lease at this address.
LAN_IP=192.168.1.10
# LAN IP of the flaky MAIN box (Minecraft/BlueMap/backups). Used by the
# commented reverse-proxy blocks in the Caddyfile. Blank if none.
MAIN_BOX_IP=192.168.1.20
# Hostname Technitium advertises for itself.
DNS_HOSTNAME=dns.example.tld

# ── Subdomains (each gets a split-horizon A record → LAN_IP) ───────────────
TRACKER_SUBDOMAIN=tracker
# Since the admin portal (2026-09-25) this names only the OLD Actual address,
# kept for one release as a redirect to actual.admin.<domain>; Actual itself is
# at actual.admin.<domain>. Same for 'dns' -> dns.admin.<domain>.
ACTUAL_SUBDOMAIN=actual
SURE_SUBDOMAIN=sure
# The admin names (admin, kuma.admin, logs.admin, dns.admin, actual.admin) and
# the household-app names (photos, jellyfin, music, ntfy) are fixed in the
# Caddyfile; each needs its label in EXTRA_SUBDOMAINS (below) for its
# split-horizon record. 'dns' and, optionally, a BlueMap subdomain proxied to
# the main box are handled in the Caddyfile / provisioning list.

# ── Caddy / TLS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Email for Let's Encrypt / ZeroSSL account (ACME). Required for public certs.
ACME_EMAIL=you@example.tld
# ── The admin portal: ONE login for every *.admin.<domain> site ────────────
# (ADMIN_PORTAL_PLAN, 2026-09-25.) admin.<domain> (Homepage), kuma.admin.,
# logs.admin. (Dozzle), dns.admin. (Technitium) and actual.admin. all sit behind
# the `admin-auth` container — a second oauth2-proxy in password mode — so one
# sign-in covers all five. It REPLACED the per-site basic_auth that guarded
# actual.<domain> and dns.<domain> (ACTUAL_BASICAUTH_* / DNS_BASICAUTH_*, now
# gone): a browser scopes basic_auth to one origin, so every site was its own
# prompt. The tracker keeps Google sign-in (oauth2-proxy, below) and never sees
# the admin cookie, which is scoped to .admin.<domain>.
#
# The sign-in username. firstboot.sh writes `USER:HASH` into
# admin-auth/htpasswd (gitignored) from these two keys. (T1.)
ADMIN_AUTH_USER=admin
# bcrypt — the same `caddy hash-password` format basic_auth used, so the
# password that already opened actual.<domain> carries over (HomeHub store
# ActualBasicAuthHash). Reference generator:
#   docker run --rm caddy:2-alpine caddy hash-password --plaintext 'yourpass'
# (Deployments driven by the Personal secret pipeline mint the plaintext AND
# derive this hash automatically — PrepDeploySecrets.ps1, 2026-07-30; the
# plaintext for the sign-in form is read back with Show-DeploySecret.ps1.)
#
# !! DOUBLE EVERY `$` IN THIS HASH !!  `$2a$14$abc…` must be written
# `$$2a$$14$$abc…`. Docker Compose interpolates `$VAR` inside .env VALUES, so
# an un-doubled bcrypt hash whose salt begins with a letter is read as a
# variable reference and replaced with NOTHING, and every sign-in fails with
# only a "variable is not set" warning to show for it. Salts are base64, so
# ~81% of generated hashes hit this — and which ones changes every time you
# regenerate. Compose collapses `$$` back to one `$`, verified end-to-end, and
# firstboot's env reader does the same. Materialize-Deploy.ps1 does this for
# you; only hand-edits need care. (Single-quoting also works, but breaks
# outright on a value containing a single quote, so `$$` is the rule
# everywhere.) (T3.)
ADMIN_AUTH_HASH=REPLACE_WITH_caddy_hash-password_OUTPUT
# The admin session cookie's encryption seed: 32 random bytes, base64url
# (HomeHub store AdminAuthCookieSecret, Generated — never typed). Generate:
#   openssl rand -base64 32 | tr -- '+/' '-_'
# ROTATING IT SIGNS EVERY ADMIN SESSION OUT — the revocation lever, since a
# session otherwise lasts oauth2-proxy's default 168h (D-A4). Deliberately NOT
# the tracker's OAUTH2_PROXY_COOKIE_SECRET: two instances, two trust
# boundaries, two secrets. (T3.)
ADMIN_AUTH_COOKIE_SECRET=REPLACE_WITH_32_RANDOM_BYTES_BASE64URL

# WHO MAY REACH THE GATED SITES AT ALL (added 2026-08-29). Every admin site,
# every household-app site (photos., jellyfin., music., ntfy.) and the LLM
# gateway are wrapped in a `remote_ip` matcher; anything outside this gets 403
# BEFORE any login, so a WAN caller is never even told there is one. This gate
# — not the router — is what keeps them off the internet (P2).
#
# `private_ranges` is Caddy's built-in shorthand for every RFC1918 block, which
# is the honest expression of "LAN or VPN, not the internet". A real deployment
# should name the LAN and the tunnel's range instead, and must EXCLUDE the
# docker bridge space (see the LLM gateway's Caddyfile block for why). Narrow it
# to a single CIDR only if you want to distinguish devices WITHIN the LAN - that
# is a different question, and the admin login is what answers it.
#
# It does NOT affect certificates: issuance is dns-01 through Cloudflare, so
# Let's Encrypt never needs an inbound path (see stack/caddy/Dockerfile).
LAN_CIDR=private_ranges

# ── oauth2-proxy (Google sign-in for the tracker subdomain) — WI-10.2 / D1/D2 ─
#
# +--------------------- OWNER MANUAL STEP (required) ---------------------+
# | Create a Google Cloud OAuth 2.0 Client BEFORE first bring-up:          |
# |  1. console.cloud.google.com -> APIs & Services -> Credentials.        |
# |  2. Configure the OAuth consent screen (External; add each allowed     |
# |     Google account as a Test user while unverified, OR publish it).    |
# |     Scopes: openid, email, profile (the slacker-tracker pattern the    |
# |     Owner trusts uses `email profile`).                                |
# |  3. Create Credentials -> OAuth client ID -> type "Web application".   |
# |  4. Under "Authorized redirect URIs" register EXACTLY:                 |
# |        https://tracker.<your-domain>/oauth2/callback                    |
# |     (substitute TRACKER_SUBDOMAIN + DOMAIN; e.g.                        |
# |        https://tracker.example.tld/oauth2/callback ).                   |
# |     "Authorized JavaScript origins" is not required (server-side flow). |
# |  5. Paste the generated Client ID + Client secret below.              |
# +------------------------------------------------------------------------+
# PIN BUMPED 2026-07-10 (review): v7.6.0 → v7.15.2. Multiple auth-bypass
# advisories were fixed between those tags (CVE-2026-34457, CVE-2026-40575,
# GHSA-7x63-xv5r-3p2x, GHSA-pxq7-h93f-9jrg, email-validation GHSA-c5c4-8r6x-56w3)
# and this is the internet-facing front door. NOT yet sim-validated at this tag:
# re-run the homehub-sim (V1) + vmtest/export-images.sh before flashing real
# hardware (pin ledger: docs/status.md).
OAUTH2_PROXY_IMAGE_TAG=v7.15.2
OAUTH2_PROXY_CLIENT_ID=REPLACE_WITH_GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID.apps.googleusercontent.com
OAUTH2_PROXY_CLIENT_SECRET=REPLACE_WITH_GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET
# 32-byte cookie encryption seed. Generate:
#   openssl rand -base64 32 | tr -- '+/' '-_'
OAUTH2_PROXY_COOKIE_SECRET=REPLACE_WITH_openssl_rand_base64_32
# Allow-list of Google accounts permitted through (Q10.5: explicit list to start;
# a remote-settable console is a deferred FUTURE knob, not built). Comma- or
# space-separated. The first-boot flow writes each entry, one per line, into
# oauth2-proxy/authenticated-emails.txt (gitignored) which the container reads.
OAUTH2_PROXY_ALLOWED_EMAILS=you@gmail.com,other-allowed@gmail.com

# ── Office-wall kiosk site (SR-016 / OI-12, RATIFIED 2026-07-29) ───────────
# The wall panel is a keyboard-less ambient display: it cannot complete a Google
# consent, so it gets its OWN Caddy site that injects its identity by source IP
# instead of asking it to sign in. Read the Caddyfile banner before changing any
# of these four — they are the whole security model.
#
# +----------------------- DO NOT FORWARD THIS PORT -----------------------+
# | WALL_PORT is served on the LAN ONLY. The router forwards :80/:443 and  |
# | NOTHING ELSE. The hostname below does NOT resolve off the LAN/tailnet  |
# | (no public record since 2026-10-15), but the panel's no-login identity |
# | is kept off the internet by (a) no forward for this port, (b) the      |
# | LAN_IP-bound publish in docker-compose.yml, and (c) the /32 match,     |
# | not by DNS - those must hold if a public record ever reappears.        |
# +------------------------------------------------------------------------+
#
# Hostname the panel loads. A subdomain of DOMAIN so the ACME cert (dns-01)
# covers it. There is NO public DNS record (no wildcard): add its bare label to
# EXTRA_SUBDOMAINS (below) so provisioning creates the split-horizon A record
# in Technitium, the only place the name resolves (LAN and tailnet).
WALL_HOST=wall.example.tld
# LAN-only HTTPS port for that site. Any high port; keep it off :443 so a
# mis-configured router forward cannot reach it. (T0 — the default is fine.)
WALL_PORT=8443
# The panel's IP, DHCP-RESERVED on its hardware MAC. The site's allow-list is
# this address and nothing else (/32 — not the LAN CIDR: guest Wi-Fi, IoT gear
# and an inbound-facing Minecraft server share that network). If the panel's
# address moves, the site 403s it — that is the intended failure direction.
PANEL_IP=192.168.1.50
# NagLight identity the kiosk site injects as X-Forwarded-User after STRIPPING
# any client-supplied one. This is the Owner's own stable Google `sub` (D-W7:
# the panel gets the full interactive view), so panel check-offs are his.
PANEL_USER_SUB=REPLACE_WITH_GOOGLE_SUB_OF_THE_PANEL_USER

# ── Technitium DNS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PINNED (Q10.9 B+): concrete version baked into the ISO payload and docker-loaded
# at first boot. This is the exact image the homehub-sim validated (V1: latest ==
# 15.2.0, amd64 manifest verified byte-identical). Bump deliberately + re-export.
TECHNITIUM_IMAGE_TAG=15.2.0
# Initial admin password (set on first boot; provision script is idempotent).
TECHNITIUM_ADMIN_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_A_STRONG_PASSWORD
# API token NAME the provisioning script creates/reuses for headless health/config.
# The token VALUE is generated by Technitium and written to provision/.token
# (gitignored) for later curl-based health checks.
TECHNITIUM_API_TOKEN_NAME=automation
# Upstream forwarders for recursion fallback / DoH. Quad9 DoH by default;
# space-separated (KEEP the double quotes when adding more — QUOTING RULE).
TECHNITIUM_FORWARDERS="https://dns.quad9.net/dns-query"
# Blocklist URLs (space-separated, quoted). Same lists Pi-hole/AdGuard consume.
TECHNITIUM_BLOCKLISTS="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/StevenBlack/hosts/master/hosts"

# ── tracker service (NagLight web container) ───────────────────────────────
# The image is built LOCALLY from a sibling NagLight checkout (Q10.2, no registry
# yet):  docker build -t naglight:local ../NagLight
# Git remote of the DATA repo (your private definitions+logs repo).
# The tracker container clones this into its /data volume on first boot and
# `git pull --rebase` on later boots. Leave blank to manage /data manually.
# NOTE: in multi-user mode (TRACKER_MULTI_USER=true) leave this BLANK and keep
# TRACKER_COMMIT=false — hosted users get per-user data dirs, git stays off (D3).
TRACKER_DATA_REMOTE=
# Identity the tracker uses for its check-off / nightly-materialize commits.
# (Quoted: contains a space — see the QUOTING RULE in the header.)
TRACKER_GIT_NAME="naglight bot"
TRACKER_GIT_EMAIL=tracker@example.tld
# When true, `tracker serve --commit` — the nightly materialize + check-offs
# commit (and, if the remote is set, push) to the data repo. Single-user only.
TRACKER_COMMIT=true
# Trusted-header multi-user mode (D3, NagLight WI-10.5). true = identity comes
# ONLY from oauth2-proxy's X-Forwarded-Email header; per-user data dir by Google
# `sub`. Keep TRACKER_COMMIT=false and TRACKER_DATA_REMOTE blank when true.
TRACKER_MULTI_USER=false
# Multi-user only: the directory whose `definitions/` seed a BRAND-NEW user the
# first time one appears. Blank = off, which is the right production default —
# a real household's definitions arrive with the person, not from a fixture.
#
# The A19 two-VM gate is why this is a knob at all. Measured 2026-08-01: a gate
# hub ran `healthy` for hours with an EMPTY /data, because the healthcheck
# probes /healthz, which is deliberately data-free (SR-040). The panel then
# renders a tracker with no items in it, and "no red drive check" looks exactly
# like "the drive check is green". The sim seed at ../sim/tracker-seed carries
# `library-mounted` and `backup-drive-mounted` — the two lanes A19 asserts on.
#
# TWO knobs because they live on opposite sides of the bind mount: _SRC is a
# host path relative to stack/, _DIR is where it lands in the container. The
# default _SRC points at a directory that ships with no definitions/, so a box
# that sets neither seeds nothing and a box that sets only _SRC still seeds
# nothing.
TRACKER_SEED_DIR=
TRACKER_SEED_SRC=./tracker-seed
# Bearer token the tracker requires on every /api/* POST (NagLight SR-030;
# SR-097). THE SAME VALUE as every poster's token knob below
# (FINANCE_TRACKER_FEED_TOKEN, AI_USAGE_FEED_TOKEN, WEIGHT_FEED_TOKEN,
# SLEEP_FEED_TOKEN, backup.env's NAGLIGHT_TOKEN, the panel's
# WALL_SHELL_FEED_TOKEN): one minted feed token, checked here. Blank = no
# check (the tracker logs auth=false). Set, a poster that sends no bearer is
# refused 401 - including NagLight's own browser drill view, which posts
# check-offs with no Authorization header.
TRACKER_API_TOKEN=
# ── One-way traceability mirror (NagLight SN-024, the Owner 2026-08-09) ──────
# A record of how ONE person's tracked items changed over time, pushed to a
# PRIVATE git repo. The ruling it is built to: the upstream service remains the
# source of truth, and git is traceability only — never a second authority and
# never a restore path. So the mirror only ever pushes; it fetches the remote's
# HISTORY once (so commits continue the chain) and never its CONTENT, and if the
# remote gains commits this box lacks it reports and stops rather than
# force-pushing or pulling.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE SETTING IT ALONGSIDE TRACKER_DATA_REMOTE ABOVE: they point in
# OPPOSITE directions. That one pulls the remote INTO /data (single-user only);
# this one pushes /data OUT. Setting both is incoherent — pick one.
#
# Blank = off, and off is the shipped default.
TRACKER_MIRROR_REMOTE=
# REQUIRED whenever the remote is set and TRACKER_MULTI_USER=true: the ONE
# identity mirrored, as its stable Google `sub` — the same value as
# NAGLIGHT_USER / PANEL_USER_SUB. With the remote set and this blank the mirror
# REFUSES TO START and logs why, deliberately: defaulting to "the data dir"
# would push every household member's private data into one repo.
TRACKER_MIRROR_USER=
# Branch (default main) and cycle period (default 1h). Hourly is the Owner's
# call — a commit per change was judged too noisy to read back.
TRACKER_MIRROR_BRANCH=
TRACKER_MIRROR_INTERVAL=
# Repo token. A GitHub fine-grained PAT scoped to `contents: write` on THAT ONE
# private repo is enough — no other scope is used, so a leak cannot reach
# anything else. Never written to .git/config, the remote URL, argv or a log.
TRACKER_MIRROR_TOKEN=

# ── Drive/Sheet config sync (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────
# OFF unless the client id/secret, token dir, public URL AND a source are all
# set. THE CLIENT ID/SECRET ARE NOT KNOBS HERE AND THERE IS NO
# TRACKER_DRIVE_CLIENT_ID / TRACKER_DRIVE_CLIENT_SECRET ANYWHERE - not in this
# file, not on the hub. Compose reaches for the OAUTH2_PROXY_* pair above
# directly, so the household has exactly ONE Google OAuth client and one
# credential to rotate. stack/weight/weight_oauth.py reads that same pair.
#
# The ONE identity the sync runs for in multi-user mode. Blank = sync OFF and
# connect/callback only; the tracker will not guess which household member's
# sheet to apply. Same value shape as TRACKER_MIRROR_USER (a Google `sub`).
TRACKER_DRIVE_USER=
# The spreadsheet `tracker seed-sheet` created. Blank until seeded.
TRACKER_DRIVE_SHEET_ID=
# A folder source INSTEAD of a sheet; normally blank. `root` is accepted only to
# bootstrap the first connect and is REFUSED as a sync source.
TRACKER_DRIVE_FOLDER_ID=

# ── Finance-Auditor — daily finance audit pipeline (SR-014, IF-004) ─────────
# OPT-IN until Finance-Auditor passes its G-Release/G-Final: enable by adding
# `finance-auditor` to COMPOSE_PROFILES (below). Image is built from the
# private sibling repo by scripts/ensure-local-images.sh, with the
# @actual-app/api version pinned to ACTUAL_IMAGE_TAG (IF-002 coupling —
# re-run the resolver with --rebuild whenever the Actual pin bumps).
FINANCE_AUDITOR_IMAGE_TAG=local
# Which ledger Finance-Auditor reads: actual | sure. The template stays actual
# (SR-099): it is the one source a bare template satisfies unattended
# (provision-actual.sh sets Actual's password at first boot), while sure also
# needs FINANCE_SURE_API_KEY, minted by hand in Sure. HomeHub's config sets sure
# for the hub (the Owner's cutover, 2026-10-22); setting actual is the rollback.
FINANCE_DATA_SOURCE=actual
FINANCE_SURE_URL=http://sure-web:3000
FINANCE_SURE_API_KEY=
# Separate non-secret Google sub; never alias FINANCE_TRACKER_FEED_TOKEN.
FINANCE_TRACKER_FEED_USER=
# The accrual gauge (FA SR-035/SR-051; SR-098). The HOST directory that holds
# serving-periods.json, mounted read-only at /config. Mount the directory,
# never the file (a replaced file detaches a file bind mount). The file names
# the household's categories and merchants, so it is never in this repo; with
# no file Finance-Auditor posts no finance gauge.
FINANCE_PERIODS_CONFIG_DIR=/etc/homehub-finance
# Expected weekly net in integer cents (the gauge's white line); the Owner's
# starting figure is $100 a week.
FINANCE_EXPECTED_WEEKLY_NET_CENTS=10000
# Sure household ledger (profile `sure`).
# SURE_IMAGE: the Owner's FORK of Sure, built locally by scripts/build-sure-image.sh
# (WSL, real Docker) - NOT ghcr.io/we-promise/sure. No registry home, so like
# naglight:local export-images.sh re-saves it every bake and firstboot verifies the
# loaded image ID against the manifest. Default keeps the `:local` suffix the
# exporter's is_local_only() keys on.
SURE_IMAGE=sure-auroleap:local
# SURE_SOURCE_REF: the ../sure branch/commit that image is built from. The fork needs
# BOTH auroleap-bank-stale-capability and auroleap-transaction-flags, so this is an
# integration branch merging them. scripts/build-sure-image.sh builds it and stamps
# homehub.source.ref; export-images.sh REFUSES an image stamped with any other ref.
SURE_SOURCE_REF=auroleap-integration
# Postgres/Redis: PLAIN tag in the knob (what compose references and what
# `docker load` can restore) + a separate DIGEST that export-images.sh verifies at
# BAKE time against the registry (it refuses the bake on a mismatch). A
# `tag@sha256:` ref cannot be used in compose: `docker save` carries RepoTags only,
# so after `docker load` the digest form does not resolve and compose would pull.
# Registry-verified immutable indexes, 2026-10-09 (see stack/sure/README.md).
SURE_POSTGRES_IMAGE_TAG=16.15
SURE_POSTGRES_IMAGE_DIGEST=sha256:ca0bd484cb98bf4b24eb1010e73fb3fcbd6714d240fbc1a10eea5b7dbecb641d
# Explicit version matching upstream latest at resolution; independently verified.
SURE_REDIS_IMAGE_TAG=8.10.2
SURE_REDIS_IMAGE_DIGEST=sha256:2c2dff791878316e3b083188be0d578423b24813cdda5de0e80ea4f7b3e6bfd6
# HomeHub T3 store keys; fill only via the secret handoff, never commit values.
SURE_SECRET_KEY_BASE=
SURE_DB_PASSWORD=
# Public fallback once FA publishes an image (same chain as TRACKER_PUBLIC_IMAGE).
FINANCE_AUDITOR_PUBLIC_IMAGE=
# Daily run time, BOX-LOCAL (TIMEZONE below governs how this is read).
FINANCE_RUN_AT=05:00
# ROLLBACK only for Finance-Auditor since the 2026-10-22 cutover: read only
# with FINANCE_DATA_SOURCE=actual (SR-099).
# Actual budget Sync ID (Actual: Settings -> Advanced -> Sync ID). Not a
# secret. OPTIONAL: empty = the auditor auto-discovers it at startup (sole
# budget file on the server; several = fatal naming them). Set only to pin
# one budget on a multi-budget server.
FINANCE_ACTUAL_SYNC_ID=
# ROLLBACK only for Finance-Auditor (read with FINANCE_DATA_SOURCE=actual), but
# keep it filled: it is also Actual's own server password, which
# provision-actual.sh sets and Actual's browser login uses.
# Actual's SERVER password. Direction reversed 2026-07-30 (A9d): minted on the
# dev PC and SET on the un-bootstrapped server by provision-actual.sh at first
# boot (no UI step); browser logins read it back via Show-DeploySecret.ps1.
# Hand-managed installs may still set it in Actual's UI and paste it here.
FINANCE_ACTUAL_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_ACTUAL_SERVER_PASSWORD
# Bearer token for the tracker's /api/feed: the same value as TRACKER_API_TOKEN.
FINANCE_TRACKER_FEED_TOKEN=REPLACE_WITH_TRACKER_API_TOKEN

# ── Management UIs — the admin portal (WI-10.11; ADMIN_PORTAL_PLAN P1/P3) ────
# Each UI is published on 127.0.0.1 ONLY and reached through its Caddy site
# under *.admin.<domain>, behind the admin login. The loopback port is the SSH
# fallback for when Caddy is the thing that is broken:
#   ssh -L <port>:127.0.0.1:<port> hub@homehub   then open http://localhost:<port>
# Change the host ports if they collide with something.
# Uptime-Kuma (generic up-checks; can POST to NagLight /api/feed).
# PINNED (Q10.9 B+): concrete tag baked into the payload (was the floating `1`).
UPTIMEKUMA_IMAGE_TAG=1.23.17
UPTIMEKUMA_PORT=3001
# Dozzle (live container log viewer; reads the docker socket read-only).
# PINNED (Q10.9 B+): concrete tag baked into the payload (was the floating `v8`).
DOZZLE_IMAGE_TAG=v8.14.12
DOZZLE_PORT=8081
# Actual's loopback port, for the SSH fallback only (it is reached day to day at
# actual.admin.<domain>). Actual had no host port before 2026-09-25, so with
# Caddy down it could not be reached at all. Not the ACTUAL_PORT inside the
# container, which compose fixes at 5006. (T0.)
ACTUAL_PORT=5006
# ntfy (OPTIONAL self-hosted push; opt-in via COMPOSE_PROFILES / `--profile ntfy`).
# A household app, not an admin tool: its site is ntfy.<domain>, outside the
# admin namespace and with no admin login (its phone app cannot sign in).
# PINNED (Q10.9 B+): concrete tag baked into the payload (was floating `latest`).
NTFY_IMAGE_TAG=v2.25.0
NTFY_PORT=8090
# Where ntfy's port is published (ADMIN_PORTAL_PLAN §5, Phase B). EMPTY = LAN_IP,
# today's LAN port. Set 127.0.0.1 once every subscriber has been re-created
# against https://ntfy.<domain> — there is no config-only way back from that
# step, so do it last. Host-side alerts follow this knob automatically. (T0.)
NTFY_BIND_IP=
# The ntfy topic host-side alerts post to (e.g. the DNS-console fence finding
# its rules out of order). Subscribe to it wherever you want to hear about the
# box. (T0.)
HOMEHUB_ALERT_TOPIC=homehub-alerts

# ── Cloudflare (Caddy's dns-01 certificates ONLY) ───────────────────────────
# The DDNS updater was RETIRED 2026-10-15: the hub publishes no public record
# and the apex is served by an external host (see docker-compose.yml where the
# ddns service used to be). The token remains because Caddy writes the
# _acme-challenge TXT records through it.
# Create a NEW scoped API token (permissions: Zone -> DNS -> Edit, only for
# this zone) at dash.cloudflare.com -> My Profile -> API Tokens.
# Do NOT reuse the old token — the one that sat in PLAINTEXT on the legacy
# Windows box's setup share is flagged for rotation (WI-9.1 secrets finding).
CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID=REPLACE_WITH_CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=REPLACE_WITH_NEW_SCOPED_DNS_EDIT_TOKEN

# ── Image tags (PINNED for reproducibility — Q10.9 B+ ALL-IMAGES) ───────────
# Every tag here is a CONCRETE version that is docker-saved into the ISO deploy
# payload and docker-loaded at first boot (vmtest/export-images.sh + firstboot.sh),
# so what boots == what the homehub-sim validated. Floating tags (`latest`, `2-alpine`,
# `v8`) are now WRONG here: they let first boot drift from the validated set. Bump
# a pin ONLY deliberately, then re-run export-images.sh and rebuild the payload.
# The full pin set + registry digests are recorded in docs/status.md.
# TAGS caddy-cloudflare, NOT caddy. Since 2026-08-08 the stack runs a Caddy built
# from stack/caddy/Dockerfile with the caddy-dns/cloudflare provider compiled in,
# because ACME's dns-01 challenge is the only one that works here: http-01 needs
# Let's Encrypt to reach this box on :80 from the public internet, and with no
# router forward NOTHING was ever issued — Caddy then served no certificate at
# all and every HTTPS site returned nothing. scripts/ensure-local-images.sh
# builds it and derives the -builder stage's version by stripping this tag's
# suffix, so keep the `<version>-<variant>` shape.
CADDY_IMAGE_TAG=2.11.4-alpine
# Local NagLight image tag (built with `docker build -t naglight:local ../NagLight`).
# Stays `local`: no registry home (Q10.2) — this is THE image the AWOW can never
# pull, so export-images.sh fails loudly if it isn't built before baking the payload.
TRACKER_IMAGE_TAG=local
# PUBLIC fallback for the tracker image (SR-006 resolver chain): NagLight is a
# PRIVATE sibling repo, so scripts/ensure-local-images.sh resolves naglight:local
# as present -> sibling build (../NagLight) -> THIS public image (pull + retag).
# Empty = no public image published yet; set (e.g. ghcr.io/<owner>/naglight:<tag>)
# once the app repo publishes, and anyone can run the sim with no sibling checkout.
TRACKER_PUBLIC_IMAGE=
ACTUAL_IMAGE_TAG=26.7.0

# ── FileBackup — the LIBRARY backup container (E2 / P1.3) ───────────────────
# CONSUMED BY THE `filebackup` ONE-SHOT AND NOTHING ELSE. That service sits
# behind the `filebackup` profile, and the profile is deliberately NOT in
# COMPOSE_PROFILES below and must never be added to it: firstboot runs a plain
# `docker compose up -d`, and a profiled-in backup service would start a
# multi-day library backup on a freshly imaged box. The only caller is
# stack/backup/library-backup.sh, on the homehub-library-backup.timer:
#     docker compose --profile filebackup run --rm -T filebackup backup
# The image still reaches the ISO payload — vmtest/export-images.sh bakes this
# profile unconditionally, the same way it bakes ntfy.
#
# Local build, no registry home (the naglight:local pattern): built from a
# sibling ../FileBackup checkout by scripts/ensure-local-images.sh, which
# stamps homehub.source.revision so export-images.sh can refuse a stale bake.
# Build it with --rebuild — the resolver SKIPS an image that already exists,
# and a hand-tagged one would short-circuit the build and leave no stamp.
FILEBACKUP_IMAGE_TAG=local
# The five HOST paths the container binds. Each is also defaulted inline in
# docker-compose.yml (an empty value would make `:/source:ro`, which breaks the
# whole compose file, not just this service) — these are the declarations, the
# compose defaults are the floor. Every path INSIDE the container is fixed by
# stack/backup/filebackup.json and derives from nothing site-specific.
#
# The library, mounted READ-ONLY into the container. Its fstab umask=0002
# already grants world-read, so the non-root container can read it as shipped.
FILEBACKUP_SOURCE=/srv/library
# Manifest/hash cache — SYSTEM disk, deliberately: small, and it must outlive
# the backup drive being unplugged.
FILEBACKUP_STATE=/var/lib/homehub-filebackup/state
# The browsable mirror (Q-FB1) and the Snapshot_<date> history, both on the
# backup drive. Q-FB5: that drive mounts uid=65532,gid=65532 so the non-root
# container can write it; the wrapper write-probes both before every run.
FILEBACKUP_BACKUP=/mnt/backup-drive/library
FILEBACKUP_CHANGES=/mnt/backup-drive/library-changes
# The container's own log directory (Backup_Global.log). System disk.
FILEBACKUP_LOGS=/var/log/homehub-filebackup
# The 7-Zip EFFORT level for files FileBackup decides to compress - 7-Zip's own
# -mx range, a single digit 0-9 (FileBackup kit revision 12, 2026-09-01). It
# never changes WHETHER a file is compressed, only how hard 7-Zip tries, and
# every level's archive restores with the same kit.
#
# 5 IS THE RULING, NOT THE IMAGE'S DEFAULT (the Owner, 2026-09-01): FileBackup
# itself falls back to 9 when this is unset, which is 7-Zip "Ultra" - the level
# that was re-packing a 4 TB library at maximum effort for a few percent. 5 is
# 7-Zip "Normal": most of the ratio for a fraction of the CPU and wall clock.
# Passed to the container as an environment variable by docker-compose.yml,
# which also defaults it to 5 inline, so a materialised .env that predates this
# knob still gets Normal rather than silently falling back to Ultra.
FILEBACKUP_7Z_LEVEL=5
# Memory ceiling for the backup container (docker `mem_limit` syntax, e.g. 3g),
# applied with an EQUAL memswap_limit so the container cannot spill into swap
# and thrash the box it is backing up. 0 = no limit, which is the shipped
# default and the behaviour before this knob existed (hub recovery C4, SR-049).
# SIZE IT FROM MEASUREMENT, NOT A GUESS: every run logs the homehub-filebackup
# slice's memory.peak ("filebackup slice memory.peak"), an upper bound for any
# one run. Set it only after a week of those (design rollout P2).
FILEBACKUP_MEM_LIMIT=0

# ── Host / timezone (used by autoinstall) ──────────────────────────────────
TIMEZONE=Etc/UTC

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# TIER-2 OPT-IN CATALOG (SR-012) — everything below configures services that are
# profile-gated (all OFF by default; Sure is ON by Owner direction 2026-10-09,
# through SURE_ENABLED below rather than a profile; see the docker-compose.yml
# tier-2 banner and
# stack/README §9). Filling these in changes NOTHING until a profile is enabled.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Which opt-in profiles start (comma-separated). This is THE enable switch:
# compose reads it from .env, so firstboot's plain `docker compose up -d` brings
# these up too — set it BEFORE building the USB and the box boots with them.
# Empty = core only. `sure` MUST NOT appear here (since 2026-10-22): Sure is ON
# by Owner direction 2026-10-09, but through SURE_ENABLED and its own unit,
# because a profile named here makes this every-boot `up -d` wait on Sure's
# database and cache before DNS, Samba and recovery are set up. The bake
# (vmtest/export-images.sh) and the resolver (scripts/ensure-local-images.sh)
# refuse a `sure` entry; firstboot warns about one.
# Photo stacks: run at most ONE of immich/photoprism;
# immich-ml is separate and heavy — leave it off on the J4125, or run it with
# IMMICH_ML_MEM_LIMIT set (below) so it can only ever OOM itself.
#   e.g. COMPOSE_PROFILES=navidrome,audiobookshelf,vaultwarden,diun,ntfy
#
# `gunmaster3` MUST NOT APPEAR HERE, AND PUTTING IT HERE IS A DEFECT rather than
# an alternative way of switching the relay on. The crossplay relay's enable
# knob is GAME_RELAY_ENABLED below; its compose `profiles:` entry exists solely
# to keep it OUT of firstboot's bulk `docker compose up -d`, and only
# homehub-gunmaster3-relay.service ever passes `--profile gunmaster3`. Naming it
# here would let the bulk `up` start a public, unauthenticated service outside
# the unit that fences it. Refused (not warned about) by verify-hub.sh
# (TC-H-T07), scripts/ensure-local-images.sh and vmtest/export-images.sh.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=

# ── Sure household ledger (SURE_ENABLED; SR-060..SR-065, SR-100) ─────────────
# THE ENABLE SWITCH, exactly `true` or off. `true` makes firstboot enable and
# start homehub-sure.service, the one thing that passes `--profile sure`; the
# same knob decides whether HomeHub's generated backup sources carry the Sure
# ledger and storage rows (ENABLED_BY=SURE_ENABLED), and whether the bake and
# the image resolver include the Sure fork. Anything else - including unset -
# leaves the unit disabled, and a unit firstboot had enabled is disabled and
# stopped. Owner default (2026-10-09): on.
# Turning it off on a running box:
#   sed -i 's/^SURE_ENABLED=.*/SURE_ENABLED=false/' .env
#   systemctl disable --now homehub-sure
SURE_ENABLED=true

# HOST directory media libraries live under (bind-mounted into the media
# services; subdirs: music/ podcasts/ audiobooks/ photos/ immich/). Docker
# creates missing dirs, but the POINT is to aim this at real storage — NOT the
# WI-10.10 backup drives (serving media would defeat their spin-down policy).
#
# CASE MATTERS when this points at a real library: the subdir names above are
# lowercase, and both ntfs3 and ext4 are case-SENSITIVE, so a `Music` folder is
# not found by a `music` bind mount — docker silently creates an empty
# lowercase sibling instead. Match the on-disk casing exactly.
#
# IF THIS PATH IS ON A MOUNTED DRIVE, it must be mounted BEFORE compose starts,
# or docker creates the bind sources on the system disk and the real mount then
# shadows them (firstboot step 3b handles this; see the note there).
MEDIA_ROOT=/srv/media

# Extra split-horizon subdomains (space/comma-separated bare labels), each gets
# an A record → LAN_IP at provisioning. Add a label when you uncomment its
# Caddyfile site — QUOTE it (QUOTING RULE): EXTRA_SUBDOMAINS="vault photos music"
# ALSO put WALL_HOST's bare label here (e.g. "wall"): the kiosk site is always
# on, so the panel needs the split-horizon record to reach it over the LAN
# rather than hairpinning through the router.
# AND GAME_HOST's bare label (e.g. "game") when GAME_RELAY_ENABLED is true —
# see the block below for why that record is worth more than convenience.
EXTRA_SUBDOMAINS=

# ── Gun Master 3 crossplay relay (GAME_RELAY_ENABLED) ────────────────────────
# The family relay so the Owner and his son can see each other in Gun Master 3
# from different houses. Source: FinnsGame/server. OFF unless
# GAME_RELAY_ENABLED=true below.
#
# ── IT IS NOT A COMPOSE PROFILE, AND THAT IS THE 2026-08-29 REFRESH ──────────
# Every other opt-in service on this box is switched on by naming its profile in
# COMPOSE_PROFILES. This one is not, because DOCKER DOES NOT OWN ITS LIFECYCLE:
# `homehub-gunmaster3-relay.service` does, and that unit `Requires=` the host
# fence (`homehub-game-isolation.service`) so that systemd — not shell branching
# in firstboot — both orders the fence first and propagates its failure. The
# compose service keeps `restart: "no"` and `profiles: ["gunmaster3"]`, which
# together mean nothing but that unit can ever start the container.
# Full reasoning: HomeHub's CROSSPLAY_HANDOFF.md §7.
#
# +--------------------- THIS ONE IS DELIBERATELY PUBLIC ------------------+
# | Every other password-guarded surface on this box was taken OFF the     |
# | internet on 2026-08-29. This one is the exception, by ruling: the      |
# | whole requirement is playing together while away from home and NOT on  |
# | the VPN. Its door is a SECRET PATH, not an identity check — the        |
# | Caddyfile's game block carries the full reasoning and the honest       |
# | account of what that is worth. Read it before changing any of these.   |
# +------------------------------------------------------------------------+
#
# THE ENABLE SWITCH. `true` makes homehub-gunmaster3-relay.service start the
# relay (behind its fence); anything else — including unset — makes that unit's
# ExecCondition report "condition not met", which systemd records as a CLEAN
# SKIP rather than a failure, so an unused relay never shows red anywhere.
# Same convention as REMOTE_UI_ENABLED and IMMICH_ML_ENABLED. (T0.)
#
# Turning it on afterwards, on a running box, is three commands and no reflash:
#   sed -i 's/^GAME_RELAY_ENABLED=.*/GAME_RELAY_ENABLED=true/' .env
#   systemctl start homehub-gunmaster3-relay
#   bash scripts/verify/verify-hub.sh   # TC-H-T06 + TC-H-T07 from HomeHub
GAME_RELAY_ENABLED=false
#
# Hostname the game connects to. A subdomain of DOMAIN so the ACME cert (dns-01)
# covers it. It needs a Technitium A record (add its label to EXTRA_SUBDOMAINS
# or provision-technitium.sh's add_a); there is no public wildcard record.
GAME_HOST=game.example.tld
#
# THE DOOR. 43 base64url characters (32 random bytes), T3-Generated: minted by
# PrepDeploySecrets.ps1 via New-RandomToken, which already emits base64url
# (+ -> -, / -> _, = stripped) so the value drops into a URL path unescaped.
# Never prompted, never displayed.
#
# THIS LINE EXISTS SO THE EMITTER HAS A TEMPLATE KEY TO FILL — a knob with no
# template line is emitted by nothing and reported by nothing (the
# OPERATOR_PASSWORD lesson, open-items E1).
#
# TREAT THE RESULTING URL AS A PASSWORD. It is a bearer token: it identifies
# nobody and works from any address. If it leaks, rotate this value,
# re-materialise, redeploy, and re-paste on each device — that is a complete
# revocation and it costs one evening's inconvenience.
GAME_WS_SECRET=REPLACE_WITH_GAME_WS_SECRET
#
# Players allowed at once; extras are told `full` and disconnected. Small on
# purpose — with no identity check, the room size IS the blast radius of a
# leaked URL. Raise it only when there is a reason. (T0.)
GAME_MAX_PLAYERS=4
#
# THE RELAY'S OWN DOOR — ruled 2026-08-30 (the Owner, open-items A29). Optional,
# and it SHIPS BLANK. Blank is safe rather than merely tolerated: the relay reads
# it as `?.trim() ?? ""` and treats empty as "no key configured -> open relay",
# which is the posture this box has had since the relay landed. It says so on
# startup, verbatim: "no connect key — open to anyone who reaches it".
#
# TWO DOORS, NOT ONE. GAME_WS_SECRET above guards the URL; this guards the
# BUILD. Set it and a leaked URL alone stops being enough to connect.
#
# ROTATION IS THE EXPENSIVE PART, which is why the game carries two keys and the
# relay only knows this one:
#   this key      -> a redeploy AND a game rebuild, in that order
#   the ROOM key  -> a game rebuild and nothing else; the relay never sees it
# The Owner's stated intent is that this one does NOT rotate. Note the trap: a
# build still carrying the old value cannot connect after a rotation, and the
# person holding that build may be a child on another machine.
#
# THREE NAMES, ONE VALUE, and they are deliberately not unified: this knob,
# GM3_CONNECT_KEY in the game build's .env, and `key` on the hello frame. The
# relay REFUSES TO START if it finds GAME_ROOM_KEY (a retired merged name) or a
# prefix-stripped CONNECT_KEY, because either would come up open while looking
# configured. (T3.)
GAME_CONNECT_KEY=
# Label in the relay's /health body and startup log. Cosmetic. (T0.)
GAME_RELAY_NAME=homehub-gunmaster3-relay
# Build tag, and it is PINNED TO A COMMIT rather than `local` (2026-08-30, the
# Owner: "tie to the sha for the currently available relay"). `local` is not a
# version: it cannot say what is running, it cannot roll back, and a rebuild
# destroys the thing you would roll back to. Every THIRD-PARTY image in this file
# carries an exact version; this makes one of the four self-built ones match.
#
# THE SHA IS THE LAST COMMIT THAT TOUCHED THE BUILD CONTEXT (`FinnsGame/server`),
# not the repo HEAD. Deliberate: the relay's bytes depend on `server/` alone, so
# pinning to repo HEAD would churn this tag every time the GAME changes and force
# rebuilds that produce identical images. ensure-local-images.sh already reasons
# this way for its `+dirty` check, which it scopes to the build context for the
# same reason.
#
# TO MOVE IT: re-pin to the new sha and rebuild, in that order —
#   git -C ../FinnsGame log -1 --format=%h -- server/     # the new value
#   ENV_FILE=<the real .env> bash scripts/ensure-local-images.sh --rebuild
# The image is also stamped `homehub.source.revision` with the repo HEAD it was
# built from, which is a different (and coarser) fact than this tag.
GAME_RELAY_IMAGE_TAG=2b760fe
#
# THE URL THE FAMILY PASTES into the game's Multiplayer box, once per device:
#     wss://<GAME_HOST>/ws-<GAME_WS_SECRET>
# With GAME_HOST's label in EXTRA_SUBDOMAINS, split-horizon makes that ONE URL
# work in both directions: at home it resolves to LAN_IP and never leaves the
# house; away it resolves to the public IP. Same string, no second profile.

# ── tier-2 image pins ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Best-effort pins as of 2026-07 authoring — NOT sim-validated (unlike the core
# pins above) and NOT baked into the ISO payload unless you export with their
# profile (vmtest/export-images.sh EXTRA_PROFILES). VERIFY the current release
# at enable time; the diun profile watches them for updates afterwards.

# Photos option A: Immich (server + ML share one version; DB image tag comes
# from Immich's release notes — bump BOTH deliberately, they move together).
IMMICH_IMAGE_TAG=v3.0.2
IMMICH_DB_IMAGE_TAG=14-vectorchord0.4.3-pgvectors0.2.0
IMMICH_REDIS_IMAGE_TAG=9
IMMICH_PORT=2283
# Phase B bind (ADMIN_PORTAL_PLAN §5). EMPTY = LAN_IP, today's LAN port; set
# 127.0.0.1 once the mobile app's server URL is https://photos.<domain> and
# auto-backup is verified. The same shape for JELLYFIN_BIND_IP and
# NAVIDROME_BIND_IP below — one app at a time. (T0.)
IMMICH_BIND_IP=
IMMICH_DB_USERNAME=immich
IMMICH_DB_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_A_STRONG_PASSWORD
IMMICH_DB_DATABASE=immich
# true ONLY when the immich-ml profile is also enabled (semantic search + faces).
IMMICH_ML_ENABLED=false
# Hard memory ceiling for the ML container (docker `mem_limit` syntax, e.g. 2g).
# On an 8 GB box this is what keeps a model load or an initial library scan from
# OOM-killing something in the CORE stack — the kernel kills this container
# instead, and compose restarts it. Empty/0 = no limit (fine on a big box).
IMMICH_ML_MEM_LIMIT=

# Photos option B: PhotoPrism (upstream publishes `latest` as THE stable tag —
# the exception to concrete pins here; diun flags when it moves).
PHOTOPRISM_IMAGE_TAG=latest
PHOTOPRISM_DB_IMAGE_TAG=11
PHOTOPRISM_PORT=2342
PHOTOPRISM_ADMIN_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_A_STRONG_PASSWORD
PHOTOPRISM_DB_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_A_STRONG_PASSWORD

# Video: Jellyfin. QSV transcode needs /dev/dri, which firstboot detects and
# wires up via a generated docker-compose.override.yml — nothing to set here.
# JELLYFIN_LIBRARY_DIR: the HOST path Jellyfin sees as /media. Empty = the whole
# ${MEDIA_ROOT}. Set it to the video subtree when the media root also holds
# music/photos that Jellyfin has no business indexing.
JELLYFIN_IMAGE_TAG=10.10.7
JELLYFIN_PORT=8096
# Phase B bind: EMPTY = LAN_IP; 127.0.0.1 once Jellyfin's published URL and the
# TV/phone apps point at https://jellyfin.<domain>. A device that ignores
# Technitium (hard-coded public DNS) cannot follow — keep the LAN port for it.
JELLYFIN_BIND_IP=
JELLYFIN_LIBRARY_DIR=

# Music: Navidrome (Subsonic API).
NAVIDROME_IMAGE_TAG=0.53.3
NAVIDROME_PORT=4533
# Phase B bind: EMPTY = LAN_IP; 127.0.0.1 once the Subsonic clients point at
# https://music.<domain>.
NAVIDROME_BIND_IP=

# Podcasts + audiobooks: Audiobookshelf.
AUDIOBOOKSHELF_IMAGE_TAG=2.17.5
AUDIOBOOKSHELF_PORT=13378

# Passwords: Vaultwarden — HTTPS-only via the Caddyfile vault.* site (no LAN
# port on purpose). Flip signups on for first-account creation, then back off.
VAULTWARDEN_IMAGE_TAG=1.33.2
VAULTWARDEN_SIGNUPS_ALLOWED=false

# Home automation: Home Assistant (monthly YYYY.M releases) + Mosquitto (MQTT).
HOMEASSISTANT_IMAGE_TAG=2026.7
MOSQUITTO_IMAGE_TAG=2.0
MOSQUITTO_PORT=1883

# Files: Syncthing (set a GUI password on first visit to the web UI).
SYNCTHING_IMAGE_TAG=1.29.2
SYNCTHING_GUI_PORT=8384

# Quality-of-life: RSS, recipes, LAN dashboard.
FRESHRSS_IMAGE_TAG=1.25.0
FRESHRSS_PORT=8082
MEALIE_IMAGE_TAG=v2.4.2
MEALIE_PORT=9925
# Homepage is the admin portal's landing page (admin.<domain>) now, not a LAN
# dashboard: loopback-published, config read-only from stack/homepage/. Add
# `homepage` to COMPOSE_PROFILES to run it.
HOMEPAGE_IMAGE_TAG=v0.10.9
HOMEPAGE_PORT=3002

# Ops: diun — update NOTIFIER for the pinned images (never auto-updates).
# Endpoint targets the ntfy container over the bridge — enable the ntfy profile
# alongside, or point this at any external ntfy server.
DIUN_IMAGE_TAG=4.29.0
DIUN_NTFY_ENDPOINT=http://ntfy:80
DIUN_NTFY_TOPIC=homehub-updates

# ── Host-level credentials: NOT read by any container ───────────────────────
# These three ride .env because it is the one file the deploy pipeline already
# delivers to the box 0600 root:root, and because host scripts already know how
# to read one key out of it without sourcing the whole thing. Nothing in
# docker-compose.yml references them.
#
# OPERATOR_PASSWORD is the hub account's UNIX password (physical console + LAN
# xrdp if anyone ever opts the desktop back in). Store key OperatorPassword;
# AUTOINSTALL_PASSWORD_HASH in user-data is derived from the same value.
#
# ADDED HERE 2026-08-27, AND IT WAS A REAL GAP: the knob existed in
# FieldSchema.psd1 and the box-side readers existed, but no template carried the
# key — and Materialize-Deploy only fills keys a template already names. So the
# emitted .env never had it, and the value on the bench box got there by hand.
# A knob with no template line is emitted by nothing and reported by nothing.
OPERATOR_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_A_STRONG_PASSWORD

# ── The two OPTIONAL FEATURES, both OFF in this repo ────────────────────────
# RULED BY THE OWNER 2026-08-27: "Deployer I'm okay with as long as it defaults
# IceDrive and remote desktop to off from its side, and gets configured to
# active from the HomeHub."
#
# These are ACTIVATION knobs. They decide what firstboot turns on; they do NOT
# decide what the image carries. Carriage is a BUILD decision (packages.optional
# .list via BAKE_OPTIONAL, and the icedrive.pin artifacts), and both halves are
# derived from ONE `Extras` declaration in HomeHub's config.homehub.psd1 so that
# they cannot disagree.
#
# THEY DISAGREED FOR A MONTH, WHICH IS WHY THE GATE EXISTS. The project told
# operators to "RDP in and sign in to IceDrive" while eleven of the AppImage's
# runtime libraries were in no image at all. Activation without carriage is an
# instruction nobody can follow; Materialize-Deploy now refuses to emit one.

# Whether firstboot installs and enables the RDP + minimal-XFCE session (SR-015)
# and, with it, the boot-time session that lets a GUI app run with nobody
# connected. `false` leaves the box headless: no X, no tcp/3389.
REMOTE_UI_ENABLED=false

# Which IceDrive client firstboot provisions, if any:
#   off       nothing. No client, no mount, no credential consumed.
#   appimage  the GUI client, autostarted inside the graphical session.
#             REQUIRES REMOTE_UI_ENABLED=true - it cannot run without a display.
#   cli       the headless CLI: no session needed, signs in once
#             non-interactively and FUSE-mounts at ICEDRIVE_MOUNTPOINT.
#             Proven working; see stack/icedrive/README.md for what it does and
#             does NOT do (it is a mount client, not a sync client).
# NEVER run both: two clients on one account fight over the same folders.
ICEDRIVE_MODE=off

# Where the CLI mounts the cloud. Ignored unless ICEDRIVE_MODE=cli.
ICEDRIVE_MOUNTPOINT=/srv/icedrive

# The IceDrive account, consumed ONCE by stack/icedrive/setup-icedrive.sh, which
# hands it to `IcedriveCLI -login/-password` at provisioning. Ruled a deploy
# secret by the Owner 2026-08-27, reversing the 2026-08-09 ruling on that
# ruling's own terms (it turned on "there is no CLI or API to hand it to").
#
# ONLY THE `cli` MODE USES THIS. The AppImage takes its credential typed into
# its own GUI over RDP and cannot be handed one from here.
#
# OPTIONAL AND BLANK BY DEFAULT. Left empty, setup-icedrive.sh installs the
# binary and stops: no sign-in, no mount, no unit enabled.
ICEDRIVE_USER=
ICEDRIVE_PASSWORD=

# ── IceDrive profile capture (reimage recovery, 2026-08-29) ──────────────────
# The key that encrypts the hub's captured IceDrive sign-in - the API token, the
# saved credential and the web-session cookies - before it rides the nightly
# backup onto the removable archive drive as the `icedrive` set.
#
# THE TWO HALVES TRAVEL ON DIFFERENT MEDIA, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN. The
# ciphertext is on the backup drive; this key is here, in a file that is 0600
# root and is materialised onto the INSTALL STICK. Either alone recovers
# nothing. A plaintext profile on a drive built to be carried away would hand a
# thief a live cloud session rather than only the documents the drive already
# holds - which is why an adversarial review made this separation a condition of
# automating the capture at all.
#
# EMPTY = the capture REFUSES TO RUN. It does not fall back to writing the
# profile in the clear, and it says so. A box with IceDrive off never reaches it.
ICEDRIVE_PROFILE_KEY=

# Panel private access: opt in only after gateway + broker provisioning and
# validate-panel-access.py succeed. See panel-access/README.md. Default legacy.
PANEL_ACCESS_ENABLED=false
PANEL_ACCESS_IMAGE=node:22-bookworm-slim
PANEL_ACCESS_MODE=read-protected
PANEL_IDLE_MS=120000
PANEL_HARD_MS=900000

# ── AI CLI service (SR-019, SN-016; A40 ratified 2026-09-09) ─────────────────
# A plain hub service — no container — that takes messages from the other
# containers and on-box jobs and answers them by running ONE headless CLI
# session on the household subscription. A container was ruled OUT because it
# delivers no containment here: the credential has to come from a home
# directory a human logged into interactively, so a container would have to
# mount that home and the isolation is punctured at the only point that
# mattered. Containment instead comes from the four knobs below plus the
# read-only registry, and every one of them is asserted by a test
# (tests/test_ai_cli_service.py, stack/ai-cli/tests/ai-cli-guards.test.sh).
#
# NO CREDENTIAL IS DECLARED HERE AND NONE EVER WILL BE. The subscription token
# is minted by `claude setup-token` inside an RDP session as AI_CLI_USER and
# lives at ~AI_CLI_USER/.claude/.credentials.json, mode 0600 — it does not
# survive a reimage, the same posture as IceDrive, and the reimage checklist
# names the re-login step.

# Off by default. `false` means the account is not created, the unit is not
# installed and nothing listens. Gated exactly like REMOTE_UI_ENABLED.
AI_CLI_ENABLED=false

# THE DEDICATED UNPRIVILEGED ACCOUNT, and it must never be `hub`. `hub` is the
# box's only account with a real shell and it carries (ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL, so a
# service running as `hub` and driving an agent chosen by a caller is arbitrary
# code execution as a passwordless-sudo user. setup-ai-cli.sh REFUSES `hub` and
# `root`, and refuses any account that has since been put in sudo/docker/adm or
# named in a sudoers rule.
AI_CLI_USER=homehub-ai

# LOOPBACK OR THE DOCKER BRIDGE, NEVER THE LAN. Validated before the socket is
# created: 127.0.0.0/8, ::1, or an address a LOCAL DOCKER BRIDGE INTERFACE
# (docker0 / br-*) is actually carrying. NOT a range: the first version of this
# guard accepted the whole of 172.16.0.0/12, and a household LAN on
# 172.20.0.0/16 sits inside that range, so "the docker bridge" admitted the LAN
# (cross-review 2026-09-09, V3). On a box with no docker bridge, only loopback
# binds. `0.0.0.0` is refused by name — it is the edit that puts the
# household's interactive credential on the LAN without looking like a LAN
# address. A hostname is refused too.
AI_CLI_BIND=127.0.0.1
AI_CLI_PORT=8791

# A SCRATCH WORKING DIRECTORY PER REQUEST lives under here: one mkdtemp child
# per request, 0700, removed on every exit path including a timeout. These CLIs
# are coding agents whose sandbox is scoped to the working directory and which
# load whatever CLAUDE.md and skills the starting tree holds, so a caller-chosen
# job must never begin in a real tree.
AI_CLI_SCRATCH_ROOT=/var/lib/homehub-ai/scratch

# Which routes a caller may name, and where they are declared. The registry is
# a CATALOG; the enable-list is the CONSENT, and an absent one is "no routes"
# rather than "every route".
AI_CLI_REGISTRY=/opt/homehub/stack/ai-cli/agents.registry.csv
AI_CLI_ENABLED_ROUTES=/opt/homehub/stack/ai-cli/routes-enabled

# Per-session timeout and the per-route cooldown applied after a timeout or a
# session with no typed result. The cooldown is per registry row and
# independent by construction, so one exhausted route never mutes another.
#
# SET THE TIMEOUT AGAINST THE MEASURED FLOOR, NOT A GUESS. `--bare` — the fast
# startup meant for scripts — ignores the OAuth token and requires an API key,
# so a service on the subscription cannot use it and pays full startup per
# call: hooks, MCP servers, skills and CLAUDE.md all load every invocation.
# Measured 2026-09-09 (dev PC, claude 2.1.201, trivial prompt, three runs):
# 2.9-4.1s wall of which 1.6-2.2s is non-model startup. See ai-cli/README.md.
AI_CLI_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=300
AI_CLI_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=120

# The pacing after a SUCCESSFUL call. The cooldown above only ever paced
# failures, so a hot loop could launch session after session on the household
# subscription; this is the small gap between successful launches of the same
# row. 0 disables it.
AI_CLI_SUCCESS_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=5

# BOUNDS FOR A SMALL ALWAYS-ON BOX (cross-review 2026-09-09). The reviewed
# version had none of these: every POST spawned a thread and a CLI process
# allowed the full timeout, with no cap on how many ran at once and no ceiling
# on the body it would read first.
#   MAX_CONCURRENT   sessions allowed to run at the same time across ALL routes
#                    (the box, not the row, is the scarce thing). Extra requests
#                    get 503 immediately rather than queueing.
#   MAX_BODY_BYTES   Content-Length ceiling, refused BEFORE a byte is read.
#   SOCKET_TIMEOUT   seconds a connection may idle, so a slow client cannot pin
#                    a handler thread.
#   MAX_CONNECTIONS  concurrent connections the server will accept at all.
AI_CLI_MAX_CONCURRENT=1
AI_CLI_MAX_BODY_BYTES=262144
AI_CLI_SOCKET_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30
AI_CLI_MAX_CONNECTIONS=8

# WHERE THE PINNED CLI BINARIES ARE FOUND, and the only place they are looked
# for. The command template names `claude` or `codex` and nothing else; the
# service resolves that name HERE rather than on the inherited PATH, and
# refuses a resolved binary that is writable by group or other. A registry row
# cannot set PATH (or HOME, or CODEX_HOME) to move the target: the Env cell is
# an allow-list. Change this only if the Owner installed the CLIs elsewhere.
AI_CLI_BIN_PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin

# AUTOMATIC CLI PROVISIONING (SR-076..078). The box installs the vendor CLIs its
# enabled consumers need (AI_USAGE_SOURCES, AI_CLI_ENABLED_ROUTES) from pinned,
# checksummed artifacts, in its own supervised unit, and retries until done. Only
# the vendor sign-ins stay with a human. `auto` provisions; `off` is an explicit
# opt-out: it disables automatic provisioning and stops the retry timer, and the
# operator then supplies the CLIs.
AI_CLI_PROVISION=auto
# Minutes between retries while provisioning is incomplete (clamped 5..1440).
AI_CLI_PROVISION_RETRY_MINUTES=15

# ── AI-usage feeder (SR-021, SN-016; IF-013) ────────────────────────────────
# Reads how much of each AI subscription has been consumed and posts it to
# NagLight as gauges. It is a plain hub service on a timer — no container, for
# the same reason the AI CLI service is not one: two of the three sources sit
# beside a credential a human minted interactively into a home directory.
#
# NO CREDENTIAL IS DECLARED HERE AND NONE EVER WILL BE, exactly as for the AI
# CLI service. The feeder READS the sign-ins the Owner already made as
# AI_USAGE_USER_ACCOUNT and never writes one — `open_for_write` refuses every
# path but the state file below, the unit mounts that home read-only, and
# tests/test_ai_usage_feeder.py runs a whole cycle inside a throwaway HOME and
# fails if a single byte under it changed.
#
# THREE SOURCES, EACH VERIFIED WITH ONE REAL CALL BEFORE ITS PARSER EXISTED
# (2026-09-09): codex via `codex app-server` JSON-RPC `account/rateLimits/read`;
# Claude via the OAuth usage endpoint with its beta header; OpenCode Go via
# GET https://opencode.ai/zen/go/v1/usage. TWO MORE READERS, OPT-IN (2026-10-11,
# plan R2): `claude2` (a second Claude account in its own CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR) and
# `gemini` (the quota call Gemini CLI's own /stats makes — written from the CLI's
# source, not from an observed response; stack/ai-usage/README.md lists what a
# first live run must confirm).

# Off by default. There is no compose profile to join (this is not a
# container), so the knob IS the gate — the same shape as AI_CLI_ENABLED and
# REMOTE_UI_ENABLED. `false` means no unit, no timer, and nothing read.
AI_USAGE_ENABLED=false

# THE ONE IDENTITY THESE GAUGES ARE POSTED FOR, as the stable Google `sub` —
# the same value as NAGLIGHT_USER / TRACKER_MIRROR_USER / TRACKER_DRIVE_USER.
# BLANK IS A REFUSAL, NOT A DEFAULT: with the feeder enabled and this empty,
# setup-ai-usage.sh refuses to install and the feeder refuses to run, both
# saying why. There is no "the only user" fallback, because a single-user box
# today is a two-user box after one oauth2-proxy login, and a usage gauge on
# the wrong person's board is a lie about their own subscription.
AI_USAGE_USER=

# The UNIX account the feeder runs as, and the account whose home the vendor
# sign-ins live in. Normally the same as AI_CLI_USER, and it must be: the
# credentials are where the interactive login put them. `hub` and `root` are
# refused. The account is created and certified by setup-ai-cli.sh — this
# feeder never creates one.
AI_USAGE_USER_ACCOUNT=homehub-ai

# WHERE THE GAUGES GO. Required; there is no default, because the tracker is
# bridge-only (no host publish) so the right value depends on this box: its
# loopback from inside, or its docker-bridge address from the host. The feeder
# REFUSES any other host — a LAN address, a public name, or the https route
# through oauth2-proxy, which would overwrite X-Forwarded-User and post the
# gauges as somebody else. Same lesson as AI_CLI_BIND: an address a bridge
# actually carries, not a range that merely contains one.
#
# THE DEFAULT IS NOW LOOPBACK, AND THAT IS A COMPOSE CHANGE, NOT A GUESS. The
# tracker publishes `127.0.0.1:8787:8787` (see stack/docker-compose.yml), which
# exists precisely so this value can be the same on every deployment. It used to
# be blank with "the right value depends on this box", because the only
# reachable address was the container's own bridge IP — which compose reassigns
# on a recreate, so it could not be written here, could not be baked into an
# image, and became a permanently unavailable gauge the first time the stack
# came up in a different order.
AI_USAGE_FEED_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787/api/feed
# Bearer token for /api/feed: the same value as TRACKER_API_TOKEN. Blank sends
# no Authorization header, which a tracker with TRACKER_API_TOKEN set refuses 401.
AI_USAGE_FEED_TOKEN=

# Which sources to read, comma-separated: codex, claude, claude2, gemini,
# opencode. An unknown name is REFUSED rather than skipped. Drop a source the
# Owner has not signed in — a permanently unavailable gauge teaches people to
# ignore unavailable gauges, which is the one thing this feeder must not do.
#
# THE ABSENT-KNOB DEFAULT IS STILL codex,claude,opencode: claude2 and gemini each
# need their own sign-in, so they never switch themselves on. The six-ring set
# the wall is built for is
#   AI_USAGE_SOURCES=claude,claude2,codex,gemini,opencode
# (FreeLLMAPI, the sixth, has no reader yet). The panel draws whatever is served,
# so changing this line and restarting homehub-ai-usage.timer is the whole edit;
# re-run setup-ai-usage.sh afterwards so the matching token keepers are
# installed (or removed).
AI_USAGE_SOURCES=codex,claude,opencode

# Where each source's credential is READ FROM (never written). Codex has no
# entry on purpose: `codex app-server` reads its own ~/.codex/auth.json in a
# child process and no token passes through the feeder at all.
AI_USAGE_CLAUDE_CREDENTIALS=/home/homehub-ai/.claude/.credentials.json
AI_USAGE_OPENCODE_AUTH=/home/homehub-ai/.local/share/opencode/auth.json

# CLAUDE ACCOUNT 2 (reader `claude2`; ids ai-usage-claude2-*). A DIFFERENT file
# from account 1's, in the CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR account 2 was signed in under. Never
# a copy of account 1's: the refresh token is single-use and rotating, so a copy
# refreshed anywhere retires the live one. Read only when `claude2` is in
# AI_USAGE_SOURCES.
AI_USAGE_CLAUDE2_CREDENTIALS=/home/homehub-ai/.claude-2/.credentials.json

# GEMINI (reader `gemini`; id ai-usage-gemini-daily). The file Gemini CLI itself
# maintains — the feeder reads `access_token` and `expiry_date` from it and
# NEVER refreshes or writes it (SR-021). Read only when `gemini` is in
# AI_USAGE_SOURCES.
AI_USAGE_GEMINI_CREDENTIALS=/home/homehub-ai/.gemini/oauth_creds.json
# THE GAUGE IS ONE BUCKET: the lowest remaining fraction among these model ids,
# comma-separated, exactly as the quota call names them. Blank = every bucket the
# call returns, and the label says "(all models)". With a list, every named model
# MUST have a bucket or the reading is refused, and the label names the list.
AI_USAGE_GEMINI_MODELS=
# Only for an account whose loadCodeAssist answer names no project (the CLI needs
# GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT for those too). Blank otherwise.
AI_USAGE_GEMINI_PROJECT=

# The `claude-code/<version>` user agent the OAuth usage endpoint is called
# with. Bump it when the CLI is upgraded on the box.
AI_USAGE_CLAUDE_VERSION=2.0.0

# The ONE file this feeder may write: the last reading per gauge, so a source
# that fails can repost the number that WAS true at the stamp it was true
# instead of inventing one. Percentages and timestamps only — asserted.
#
# IT MUST STAY INSIDE THE SERVICE'S OWN StateDirectory (/var/lib/homehub-ai).
# The write guard resolves symlinks and refuses anything outside that
# root, so a path aimed at a credential is refused however this line is
# edited; a path merely moved elsewhere costs the feeder its history and
# says so in the journal rather than failing quietly.
AI_USAGE_STATE_FILE=/var/lib/homehub-ai/usage-state.json

# Per-source timeout, whole seconds. A wedged source must become an
# "unavailable" gauge, not a stuck unit.
AI_USAGE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# HOW OFTEN EACH SOURCE IS READ, whole seconds, 60..600 (SR-095). The timer runs
# every minute; a source is read only when its own interval has passed, and a
# vendor answering 429 or 5xx backs that source off on its own (Retry-After
# honoured, else doubling to 10 minutes). Blank is the default: 60 for every
# HTTP source, 120 for Codex, whose `codex app-server` costs ~1.5 s of CPU per
# read. Set a source back to 600 -- the old cadence -- if its vendor ever objects.
AI_USAGE_CLAUDE_INTERVAL=
AI_USAGE_CLAUDE2_INTERVAL=
AI_USAGE_CODEX_INTERVAL=
AI_USAGE_GEMINI_INTERVAL=
AI_USAGE_OPENCODE_INTERVAL=

# ── Weight feeder (SR-022, SN-040; IF-014) ──────────────────────────────────
# Posts one body-weight reading as the `weight` gauge, against the goal the
# person declared in THEIR OWN definitions. It REPLACES NagLight's manual
# feeders/weight-manual.ps1 by posting the same gauge id, so the two never
# stand two bars side by side. A plain hub service on a timer, no container —
# the vendor half is a refresh token a human minted through a browser.
#
# THE VENDOR HALF IS BLOCKED ON AN OWNER ACTION AND THE FEEDER SAYS SO. Google
# Health API v4 was verified against Google on 2026-09-09 (the API, the Weight
# type, the route and the scope — see stack/weight/README.md for the calls and
# the responses), but no authenticated call has ever been made, so the feeder
# contains NO PARSER and posts "unavailable" until one has. That is correct
# behaviour, not a fault to chase.
#
# NOTE THE SCOPE IS NOT WEIGHT-SPECIFIC. The only scope that admits the Weight
# data type is googlehealth.health_metrics_and_measurements.readonly, which
# also grants blood glucose, body fat, oxygen saturation and heart-rate
# metrics. Consenting to it hands this box all of those.

# Off by default. There is no compose profile to join (this is not a
# container), so the knob IS the gate — the same shape as AI_USAGE_ENABLED.
WEIGHT_ENABLED=false

# THE ONE IDENTITY THIS GAUGE IS POSTED FOR, as the stable Google `sub` — the
# same value as NAGLIGHT_USER / TRACKER_DRIVE_USER / AI_USAGE_USER. BLANK IS A
# REFUSAL, NOT A DEFAULT, and it matters more here than for a usage gauge: a
# body weight on the wrong household member's board is a private measurement
# displayed to the wrong person on a wall in a shared room.
WEIGHT_USER=

# The UNIX account the feeder runs as, and whose home the refresh token lives
# in. Unlike the usage feeder this account is NOT shared with the AI CLIs:
# this credential is minted for this purpose, so it gets a home with nothing
# else in it. Created by setup-weight.sh. `hub` and `root` are refused.
WEIGHT_USER_ACCOUNT=homehub-weight

# WHERE THE GAUGE GOES. Required; there is no default, because the tracker is
# reachable on the hub's loopback because the tracker publishes
# `127.0.0.1:8787:8787` (see stack/docker-compose.yml and the note on
# AI_USAGE_FEED_URL, which this shares). The feeder REFUSES any other host — a
# LAN address, a public name, or the https route through oauth2-proxy, which
# would overwrite X-Forwarded-User and post the reading as somebody else.
WEIGHT_FEED_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787/api/feed
# Bearer token for /api/feed: the same value as TRACKER_API_TOKEN. Blank sends
# no Authorization header, which a tracker with TRACKER_API_TOKEN set refuses 401.
WEIGHT_FEED_TOKEN=

# WHERE THE GOAL LIVES, AND IT IS NOT A KNOB ON THIS BOX. SN-040: "the goal
# lives in the user's definitions so it syncs like everything else". So this
# names the DIRECTORY, not the number - in multi-user mode it is
#   <tracker data root>/<the Google sub>/definitions
#
# THE GOAL IS THE `target` OF ONE ITEM IN THERE. As of 2026-09-09 the feeder
# reads it from the weigh-in item's own `target:`, with `unit: lb` beside it:
#
#   category: Health
#   items:
#     - id: weigh-in
#       title: Step on the scale
#       type: habit
#       recur: weekly
#       horizon: long
#       target: 170
#       unit: lb
#
# IT USED TO BE A TOP-LEVEL `weight_goal_lb:` KEY AND THAT DID NOT SURVIVE THIS
# HOUSEHOLD. Definitions reach the hub two ways. In Drive FOLDER mode the .md
# bytes are staged verbatim and any key rides along. In SHEET mode - which this
# household runs, TRACKER_DRIVE_SHEET_ID set and FOLDER_ID deliberately blank -
# internal/defsheet REGENERATES the .md from a fixed ITEM-column list and drops
# unknown columns, so a top-level key is erased by the first sync after anyone
# edits the sheet. `target` and `unit` are already item columns that round-trip
# today. A file still carrying the old key is REFUSED with a message saying
# where the number goes; if both are present the feeder refuses rather than
# picking one, because silent precedence is how a person ends up looking at a
# bar drawn around a number they thought they had changed.
#
# THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO WEIGHT_GOAL KNOB TO FALL BACK ON. A goal on the hub
# would need an SSH session and a redeploy to change, would not travel with the
# rest of the person's tracker, and would be a second home for the household's
# intent - which is how this repo's /opt/homehub drift started. With no goal
# declared the feeder posts NOTHING and says which item to put it on.
WEIGHT_DEFINITIONS_DIR=


# WHICH ITEM CARRIES THE GOAL. A LOCATION, NEVER A NUMBER - that distinction is
# the acceptance criterion, not a nicety: neither of these can hold a weight, so
# the household's intent still lives only in the person's own definitions and
# still syncs with them. They are knobs so that renaming the item, or moving it
# to another category, is an .env edit rather than a code change. Blank takes
# the default, which is the shape the Owner's sheet already syncs. The match is
# case-insensitive and trimmed on both halves, because both are typed by hand
# into a spreadsheet cell.
WEIGHT_ITEM_CATEGORY=Health
WEIGHT_ITEM_ID=weigh-in

# ── The waist-to-height sister gauge (NI_A2). SHIPS OFF. ────────────────────
# Turn this on only AFTER adding the waist item to the tracker sheet (the exact
# row is in stack/weight/README.md). It gates a second gauge, `weight-waist`,
# that stands beside the weight bar in the same panel cell.
#
# IT IS OFF BY DEFAULT FOR TWO REASONS BEYOND HOUSE STYLE. It costs two extra
# calls every cycle - a Google Health list for the height and a GET /api/today
# for the waist - which a hub whose Owner never added the item would pay forever
# to learn nothing. And the sheet row is a MANUAL step, so "true" here is a
# statement that a person has done it; an automatic default would leave the
# feature in a permanent, silent half-failure on every other box.
#
# NO NEW CONSENT IS ASKED FOR. The height comes from the SAME Google Health
# route with the data type swapped (`dataTypes/height/dataPoints`) under the
# SAME scope the weight read already holds - there is no height-specific scope
# any more than there is a weight-specific one. The waist comes from NagLight's
# own GET /api/today on the destination WEIGHT_FEED_URL already verified, so
# nothing new is mounted, granted or exposed.
WEIGHT_WAIST_ENABLED=false

# WHICH ITEM CARRIES THE WAIST. A LOCATION, NEVER A NUMBER - WEIGHT_ITEM_* 's
# rule exactly. The default category is the weigh-in's ON PURPOSE: setup-weight.sh
# binds only the ONE category file the goal lives in into the service, so keeping
# both items under `Health` means the waist target is readable through a mount
# that already exists. Moving the item to another category needs that bind
# widened too, and if it is not, the feeder quietly uses the documented default
# target (0.5) rather than failing.
WEIGHT_WAIST_CATEGORY=Health
WEIGHT_WAIST_ITEM_ID=waist-in

# Which source to read. Unknown names are REFUSED rather than skipped.
WEIGHT_SOURCE=google-health

# The Google Health OAuth refresh token, READ and never written BY THE FEEDER.
# It is minted interactively by the Owner at a browser and does NOT survive a
# reimage. The minting tool is stack/weight/weight_oauth.py (`mint`), run once
# over SSH with sudo; stack/weight/README.md carries the exact commands, the
# one redirect URI to add in the Google console, and what each step prints.
# Its own directory, outside the state file's, for the same reason
# TRACKER_DRIVE_TOKEN_DIR is its own volume rather than a path under /data:
# a token in a directory something else sweeps up gets swept up.
WEIGHT_TOKEN_FILE=/var/lib/homehub-weight/tokens/google-health-token.json

# The ONE file this feeder may write: the last reading, so a source that fails
# can repost the number that WAS true at the stamp it was true instead of
# inventing one. A weight and a timestamp only — asserted by test.
#
# IT MUST STAY INSIDE THE SERVICE'S OWN StateDirectory (/var/lib/homehub-weight).
# The write guard resolves symlinks and refuses anything outside that
# root, so a path aimed at a credential is refused however this line is
# edited; a path merely moved elsewhere costs the feeder its history and
# says so in the journal rather than failing quietly.
WEIGHT_STATE_FILE=/var/lib/homehub-weight/weight-state.json

# Source timeout, whole seconds. A wedged source must become an "unavailable"
# gauge, not a stuck unit.
WEIGHT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# ── Sleep feeder (SR-070..SR-072; plan 4.3) ─────────────────────────────────
# Posts the CPAP's own ResMed myAir sleep score as the `sleep` gauge. A plain hub
# service on a 30-minute timer, no container. Nothing is computed: the number is
# myAir's. stack/sleep/README.md has the data contract and what is not proven.
#
# THE JOURNAL CARRIES STATUS ONLY ("published night <date>"): never a score or
# any health value. The score lives in the state file, mode 0600.

# Off by default. There is no compose profile to join (this is not a container),
# so the knob IS the gate - the same shape as WEIGHT_ENABLED. A literal `true`
# makes first boot (firstboot.sh step 6f-2) run stack/sleep/setup-sleep.sh; the
# hub's deploy config (HomeHub config.homehub.psd1) sets it, this template stays
# off. On a RUNNING hub, stack/sleep/README.md has the in-place procedure.
SLEEP_ENABLED=false

# THE ONE IDENTITY THIS GAUGE IS POSTED FOR, as the stable Google `sub` - the same
# value as NAGLIGHT_USER / AI_USAGE_USER / WEIGHT_USER. BLANK IS A REFUSAL, not a
# default: a sleep score on the wrong household member's board is a private health
# measurement shown to the wrong person on a wall in a shared room.
SLEEP_USER=

# The UNIX account the feeder runs as. It has NO home and holds no credential
# file. Created by setup-sleep.sh. `hub` and `root` are refused.
SLEEP_USER_ACCOUNT=homehub-sleep

# WHERE THE GAUGE GOES (loopback tracker port, same rule as AI_USAGE_FEED_URL: the
# feeder REFUSES any other host, and refuses redirects). The bearer token is the
# same value as TRACKER_API_TOKEN; blank sends no Authorization header.
SLEEP_FEED_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787/api/feed
SLEEP_FEED_TOKEN=

# PUBLICATION. 1 (the default) puts the score on the wall from the day the feeder
# is deployed (plan decision 5: no review period, no tuning pass). 0 (or false,
# no, off) is an operator OFF SWITCH and nothing more: the feeder reads nothing
# and posts nothing.
SLEEP_PUBLISH=1

# THE myAir SIGN-IN. The email is a private identity value (T1): it reaches this
# file from the identity table (@identity:household.MyAirEmail in
# config.common.psd1). The password is a secret (T3): it reaches this file from
# the secret store (key MyAirPassword) through the materialiser. NEITHER IS EVER
# LOGGED, ECHOED OR WRITTEN BY THE FEEDER. Blank = the feeder refuses to start.
# The materialiser doubles every `$` in a value and systemd does not undo it; the
# feeder collapses `$$` itself. A password containing a backslash or a double
# quote is not safe through systemd's EnvironmentFile= and is not supported.
MYAIR_EMAIL=
MYAIR_PASSWORD=

# The ONE file this feeder may write: the nightly scores (night key, score and a
# frozen observed_at), the consecutive-sign-in-failure count and a sticky marker.
# NEVER a credential, a token or a response body - asserted by test. It must stay
# inside the service's own StateDirectory (/var/lib/homehub-sleep); the write
# guard resolves symlinks and refuses anything outside it.
SLEEP_STATE_FILE=/var/lib/homehub-sleep/sleep-state.json

# Per-request timeout, whole seconds (a cycle is five HTTPS calls). A wedged
# source must become an "unavailable" gauge, not a stuck unit.
SLEEP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# The night keys, the 14:00 "unavailable" rule and the 12:00 stamp are all read
# in the hub's zone, which is the TIMEZONE knob above (Host / timezone). The
# feeder refuses a blank or unknown zone rather than guessing one.

# ── Heart gauge: daily RESTING HEART RATE (H1; SR-073, IF-014) ───────────────
# A third gauge from the SAME feeder and the SAME Google Health connection: one
# number per day, in bpm, lower is better (`heart`, unit `bpm`, daily window).
# It needs NO new consent: `daily-resting-heart-rate` is listed under the scope
# the weight read already holds. It is built ahead of a data source - until a
# Fitbit-family device writes to Google Health it posts "unavailable" (a dash)
# and logs nothing. See "Heart (resting heart rate)" in stack/weight/README.md.
#
# OFF BY DEFAULT: only a literal 1 or true turns it on, and only while
# WEIGHT_ENABLED=true. Off means nothing is fetched and nothing is posted.
# When on it also needs TIMEZONE (below) set to the hub's real zone: each day is
# stamped at 12:00 LOCAL so it lands in NagLight's 02:00-local tracking day. An
# unset or unknown TIMEZONE skips the heart gauge (weight is unaffected).
HEART_ENABLED=0
# WHERE THE NUMBER COMES FROM (ruled 2026-10-25, HomeHub
# docs/HEART_GAUGE_PLAN_2026-10-25.md). `samples`: the hub computes each night's
# resting rate from the watch's 5-minute `heart-rate` samples (the lowest
# 30-minute average while asleep, blips dropped) and colours it against the
# median of your earlier nights. `daily`: Google's own daily resting rate,
# which only Fitbit-family devices get. Blank means `daily`.
HEART_SOURCE=samples
# samples mode: the device whose samples count (dataSource.device.manufacturer),
# the target used until HEART_MIN_BASELINE_NIGHTS valid nights exist (30..150),
# the bar's width centred on the target (even, 6..120; 24 puts yellow at +4 and
# red at +12), and the baseline window in nights. Each refused when malformed.
HEART_SAMPLE_DEVICE=FFit
HEART_TARGET_BPM=75
HEART_COLOUR_SPAN_BPM=24
HEART_BASELINE_NIGHTS=28
HEART_MIN_BASELINE_NIGHTS=7
# samples mode: WHERE the long-term goal row lives in the Sheet (2026-10-25).
# The goal itself is that row's `target` (bpm) and `due` (the date to reach it),
# so it is changed or reset from the Sheet, never here. No row: no goal path.
HEART_GOAL_CATEGORY=Health
HEART_GOAL_ITEM_ID=heart-goal
# daily mode only: the bar and the standing line, whole bpm (each 1..300, MIN <
# MAX, MIN <= TARGET <= MAX; TARGET is shared with samples mode above). A bad
# value skips the heart gauge with the knob named; the weight gauge is
# unaffected.
HEART_MIN_BPM=40
HEART_MAX_BPM=100

# ── Mesh VPN subnet router (SR-042, SN-042) ──────────────────────────────────
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: this site is behind carrier-grade NAT. Measured 2026-09-18 -
# the fibre ONT is a bridge, the router's WAN interface holds an RFC1918
# address, and an HTTPS request to the apex from a cellular client timed out. No
# inbound path can be created from this side, so the router's WireGuard server -
# correctly configured and still in place - is structurally unreachable. An
# outbound-initiated tunnel is the only remote access that works without the ISP
# handing out a public address.
#
# NOT A COMPOSE SERVICE. It is a host service installed by
# stack/tailscale/setup-tailscale.sh, because a subnet router edits the host
# routing table and forwarding sysctls and must be up before the stack is - if
# the tunnel depended on docker, a docker fault would also be what stops you
# reaching the box to fix it.
#
# OUTSIDE THE OFFLINE ISO CLOSURE, like the AI CLIs (SN-016): the daemon is not
# in the base archive, so a reimage does NOT restore it and re-authorisation is
# manual. REIMAGE_PERSISTENCE_PLAN.md carries that.
TAILSCALE_ENABLED=false

# The LAN advertised as a subnet route, so hosts that will never run a tunnel
# client - the panel, mini-serv - are reachable THROUGH this box.
#
# THIS IS THE LAN ALONE, NOT LAN_CIDR. LAN_CIDR is an allow-list and carries the
# tunnel range too; advertising the tunnel's own space as a subnet route would
# tell the tunnel to route its own addresses through this host, which is a loop.
# setup-tailscale.sh refuses a value containing the tunnel range for that reason.
TAILSCALE_ADVERTISE_ROUTES=192.168.1.0/24

# Node name in the tunnel. Kept short because it appears in every address you
# will ever type over the tunnel.
TAILSCALE_HOSTNAME=homehub

# Offer this box as a Tailscale EXIT NODE (true|false; anything else is refused).
# OFF by default. true only OFFERS it: nothing routes through the hub until the
# Owner approves "Use as exit node" for it in the admin console, and then only
# for a device that selects it (Exit node -> homehub; back with None).
# setup-tailscale.sh converges it with `tailscale set`, never `up`, reads the
# result back from `tailscale debug prefs`, and fails if the LAN route above has
# gone. Setting it back to false is the hub half of a switch-off: set every
# device to None FIRST, or one still pointing here loses its internet.
# Plan: HomeHub docs/EXIT_NODE_PLAN_2026-09-22.md. The hub's config sets true.
TAILSCALE_ADVERTISE_EXIT_NODE=false

# ── Centralised LLM gateway: FreeLLMAPI (SR-043, SN-043) ─────────────────────
#
# Enable with COMPOSE_PROFILES=...,llm-gateway. Off by default.
#
# PINNED BY DIGEST, NEVER A TAG. This service holds every provider credential
# the household has; an image that can change underneath it without a commit is
# the failure the pin prevents. The digest, its version and its date live in
# stack/llm-gateway/gateway-image.pin - change them together, in one commit.
#
# At pinning time `latest` resolved to a DIFFERENT digest than `v0.3.0`, because
# `latest` follows `main` rather than the newest release. Anyone who "pinned
# latest" would be tracking the development branch of a credential concentrator.
LLM_GATEWAY_IMAGE_DIGEST=sha256:04ce170ae9b6f209c6f66ccd1ff8dbd28b95dbd3fea202f4039e527ca26870fe

# Hostname for the Caddy site. A subdomain of DOMAIN; provision-technitium.sh
# adds the split-horizon A record (no public wildcard exists, so it resolves
# only on the LAN/tailnet); the LAN_CIDR remote_ip gate is what keeps it
# LAN+tunnel only, exactly as it does for actual.* and dns.*.
LLM_GATEWAY_SUBDOMAIN=llm

# Provider keys, the at-rest encryption key, the bearer key and the dashboard
# login live in stack/llm-gateway/llm-gateway.env - gitignored, materialised by
# the deploy tooling, never in this file and never in the compose file.

# ── Dev-PC wake and readiness (SR-044, SN-044) ───────────────────────────────
#
# devpc-wake is a SYSTEMD UNIT on the host, not a compose service - it opens a
# broadcast UDP socket, which a bridged container cannot usefully do. This knob
# is what firstboot reads to decide whether to ENABLE homehub-devpc-wake.service;
# there is no `devpc-wake` compose profile and naming one does nothing. (This
# line said "Enable with COMPOSE_PROFILES=...,devpc-wake", which was wrong and
# would have left an operator believing the service was running.)
#
# LEAVE IT false UNTIL THE PHYSICAL WAKE TEST PASSES from S3 and again from S5.
# The unit is installed either way so the test can be run with one systemctl
# command; enabling it before the packet is proven to wake the box makes every
# cold request wait out the hold cap before failing.
DEVPC_WAKE_ENABLED=false

# The dev PC's LAN address and the port its OpenAI-compatible inference server
# listens on. That server MUST run as a Windows SERVICE: SR-044 wakes the machine
# to a login screen where nobody is logged in, and a session-scoped server is
# simply absent in exactly the state this whole arrangement exists to serve.
DEVPC_HOST=192.168.1.20
DEVPC_INFERENCE_PORT=11434

# The model that must be REPORTED LOADED for the state to be `ready`.
# `up` and `ready` are different: a woken box answers ICMP tens of seconds before
# it can answer a prompt, because the weights still have to reach VRAM. A caller
# told `ready` when the truth is `up` will block and then blame the model.
#
# SET THIS TO AN ALIAS, NOT A MODEL NAME. Use `homehub-local`.
#
# The dev PC serves in one of two ways - Ollama with the model resident in VRAM,
# or llama-server with mixture-of-experts weights offloaded to system RAM for a
# model bigger than the card. THIS SIDE MUST NOT KNOW WHICH, and the mechanism
# that delivers that is a stable alias: both advertise the SAME id in
# /v1/models, so swapping tiers - or swapping the underlying model within a
# tier - changes nothing here.
#
# Put a real model name here (`qwen3:14b`) and it works until the day the model
# changes, at which point devpc-wake correctly reports a box that never becomes
# `ready`, and the cause is on a different machine from the symptom.
#
# The dev-PC half is HomeHub/scripts/devpc/ (`-ModelAlias`, default
# `homehub-local`).
DEVPC_TARGET_MODEL=homehub-local

# THE PHYSICAL ADAPTER'S MAC, and this is the trap on this box. The NIC is bound
# to a Hyper-V EXTERNAL vSwitch and the host's LAN address lives on a vEthernet
# adapter, NOT on the NIC - so resolving the MAC from DEVPC_HOST returns the
# WRONG target and wakes nothing. Take it from the physical adapter.
#
# AND THE ARMING EVIDENCE IS NOT EVIDENCE: the driver reports every wake filter
# enabled whether or not the vSwitch consumes the magic packet first. This path
# is UNVERIFIED as of 2026-09-18 and is proven only by a physical test from S3
# and again from S5.
DEVPC_WAKE_MAC=

# Hard deadline for the one estimated state. `waking` becomes `failed` here and
# never persists - a state that can stay `waking` forever never tells a caller to
# stop waiting.
DEVPC_WAKE_DEADLINE_SECONDS=90

# How long a request for the local model is HELD while the wake runs, before it
# is refused. The holder is litellm (stack/litellm/devpc_hold_hook.py), NOT the
# gateway - this line said "the gateway" when one component was going to do both
# the holding and the falling back, and it no longer does.
#
# WHAT HAPPENS AT THE CAP DEPENDS ON WHICH LANE YOU CAME THROUGH, and that is
# the whole design: litellm always REFUSES (correct for finance - substituting
# a cloud provider is the failure being prevented), and llm-gateway, if the
# request came through it, reads that refusal as retryable and walks to a free
# tier (correct for agents). Held-then-fell-back is a property of the system,
# not of any one component.
DEVPC_HOLD_CAP_SECONDS=120

# Idle period with no local-model request after which the hub PUBLISHES a
# sleep-permission verdict. The hub never actuates: the dev PC polls the verdict
# and suspends itself, re-checking its own in-flight work first - which a remote
# command could not do, being evaluated when sent and applied when it arrives.
DEVPC_IDLE_SLEEP_MINUTES=30

# Session-attachment signal (IF-025). A reading older than this is discarded.
# FAIL CLOSED MEANS ATTACHED: absent, stale, malformed or unreachable all inhibit
# sleep. A false `attached` costs idle watts until the next poll; a false
# `detached` suspends the machine mid-session and loses work.
DEVPC_SESSION_STALENESS_SECONDS=120

# Where the dev PC publishes its own session-attachment reading (IF-025), read
# by devpc-wake to decide whether it may permit sleep. EMPTY means no reading,
# which fails closed to `attached` and therefore NEVER permits sleep - the safe
# default, and the correct one until the service-mode session agent exists.
# Note the producer must be a SERVICE-MODE agent: the remote-desktop product
# sees only its own sessions and cannot see a LOCAL CONSOLE session, so sitting
# at the physical keyboard would read as detached.
DEVPC_SESSION_URL=

# ── litellm — the pinned private route to the dev PC (SR-047) ────────────────
#
# ENABLE WITH LITELLM_ENABLED BELOW - **NEVER** BY ADDING `litellm` TO
# COMPOSE_PROFILES. This line said the opposite until 2026-09-19 and the
# contradiction was live for several revisions: the profile route starts the
# container through firstboot's bulk `docker compose up -d`, which runs BEFORE
# the egress fence has ever been applied in that boot. An operator following
# the old instruction would have got a proxy with 32 provider pass-through
# routes and unrestricted outbound networking, and nothing would have said so.
#
# THIS IS THE LANE THAT CANNOT REACH A CLOUD PROVIDER, and it is that by having
# no cloud credential rather than by any rule. Its whole configuration is
# stack/litellm/config.yaml, in git, with exactly one deployment. The other
# lane (llm-gateway) keeps the twelve-platform free-tier catalogue and reaches
# the dev PC by pointing its custom endpoint at this container, so the
# wake-and-hold logic exists once. See stack/litellm/README.md.
#
# Pinned by digest for the same reason the gateway is, for a different reason:
# this process is what enforces the guarantee, so it must not change without a
# commit. stack/litellm/litellm-image.pin carries the version and the date.
LITELLM_IMAGE_DIGEST=sha256:d295634e09c648dcdb72c4cc2dd226f5fb87823a73e88cbbed6f205e4deb044b

# THE ADDRESS THE EGRESS FENCE NAMES. Must lie inside the `llmprivate` subnet
# pinned in docker-compose.yml (172.28.91.0/24) and must not collide with the
# gateway docker assigns at .1.
#
# WHY A STATIC ADDRESS AT ALL. The one-deployment config does NOT prevent this
# container reaching a cloud provider - measured against the pinned image, it
# registers 32 provider pass-through routes independent of model_list, and the
# vertex handler forwards CALLER-SUPPLIED credentials. The real boundary is
# stack/llm-isolation/, which programs DOCKER-USER to allow THIS ADDRESS to
# reach the dev PC's inference port and reject everything else. A rule needs an
# address it can name; a dynamically-allocated one moves and the rule then
# silently protects nothing.
LITELLM_CONTAINER_IP=172.28.91.10

# ENABLE THE PRIVATE LANE HERE - **NEVER** BY ADDING `litellm` TO
# COMPOSE_PROFILES. The container carries `restart: "no"` because the egress
# fence that limits it to the dev PC is installed by a systemd unit it is
# started by; naming the profile here lets firstboot's bulk
# `docker compose up -d` start the proxy OUTSIDE that fence. That is the
# gunmaster3 defect and this stack has now met it twice.
#
# LITELLM_ENABLED governs only whether firstboot ENABLES the lane unit. The
# FENCE unit is enabled either way: a fence with no container is harmless - it
# programs REJECT rules for an address nothing is using - while a container
# with no fence is a proxy with 32 provider pass-through routes and
# unrestricted outbound networking.
LITELLM_ENABLED=false

# OUR bearer key for this proxy - not a provider credential, and deliberately
# NOT the gateway's. Two lanes with one key would mean a request misdirected
# between them succeeds against the wrong one instead of 401-ing, which is the
# failure this key exists to make loud. Materialised by the deploy tooling.
LITELLM_MASTER_KEY=

# The readiness oracle devpc_hold_hook.py asks before letting a request through
# (IF-023). EMPTY DISABLES THE HOLD and is the right setting until the wake path
# is physically verified from S3 and S5: with no hold, a cold box fails in
# seconds; with a hold and a wake that does not work, every cold request waits
# out DEVPC_HOLD_CAP_SECONDS first. Set it to the devpc-wake service once the
# physical test passes:
#     DEVPC_WAKE_URL=http://172.28.91.1:8799
#
# THE LITERAL ADDRESS, NOT `host.docker.internal`, AND NOT `host-gateway`.
# Two wrong answers were tried here before this one:
#
#   * `host.docker.internal` is provided by Docker Desktop and NOT by Docker
#     Engine on Ubuntu, which is what the hub runs. The probe would fail name
#     resolution and report "no oracle".
#   * `extra_hosts: host.docker.internal:host-gateway` resolves to the DEFAULT
#     bridge's gateway - docker0, measured as 172.17.0.1 on this hub - and NOT
#     to this network's own .1. The probe would then be rejected by our OWN
#     INPUT fence, which permits 172.28.91.1 alone, and report "no oracle"
#     again. Both halves individually correct; the pair wrong.
#
# 172.28.91.1 is the .1 of the `llmprivate` subnet pinned in
# docker-compose.yml, it is what llm-isolation.sh derives for its INPUT allow
# rule, and TC-1034 asserts those three agree. devpc-wake listens on the HOST,
# which is why this crosses the bridge at all.
DEVPC_WAKE_URL=

# How often the hold hook re-asks, and how long it waits for each answer.
# HOW LONG THE RESIDENCY PROBE MAY WAIT, and why it dwarfs DEVPC_PROBE_TIMEOUT.
# devpc-wake proves `ready` with a ONE-TOKEN COMPLETION, not a model listing:
# both Ollama and llama-server list a model that is configured and NOT resident
# in VRAM, so a listing-based `ready` meant the first request after a wake still
# paid a full model load. A cold load is tens of seconds, and the point is to
# WAIT for it rather than declare the box unready and re-ask for ever.
# It never blocks a caller - the probe runs in the background.
DEVPC_READY_PROBE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60

# How long a verified residency is trusted before it must be re-proved. Short
# enough that a model the keep-alive has since evicted stops being advertised as
# ready; long enough that a burst of requests does not re-verify on every one.
DEVPC_READY_TTL_SECONDS=120

DEVPC_HOLD_POLL_SECONDS=3
DEVPC_HOLD_PROBE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=5

# ── Self-hosted remote desktop (SR-045, SN-045) ──────────────────────────────
#
# ENABLE WITH RUSTDESK_ENABLED BELOW - **NEVER** BY ADDING `rustdesk` TO
# COMPOSE_PROFILES. Both containers carry `restart: "no"` because the INPUT-chain
# fence that limits them to the LAN and the tunnel is installed by a systemd
# unit they are started by; naming the profile here lets firstboot's bulk
# `docker compose up -d` start the listeners OUTSIDE that fence. That is exactly
# the gunmaster3 defect, which scripts/ensure-local-images.sh refuses by name.
# homehub-rustdesk.service passes `--profile rustdesk` itself, which is what
# makes the profile unnecessary here.
#
# RUSTDESK_ENABLED governs only whether firstboot ENABLES the listener unit. The
# FENCE unit is enabled either way: a fence with no listeners is harmless - it
# programs REJECT rules for ports nothing is bound to - while listeners with no
# fence are the failure the whole arrangement exists to prevent.
RUSTDESK_ENABLED=false
#
# HOST NETWORKING, so these are HOST sockets - which is why the fence is in the
# INPUT chain and NOT in DOCKER-USER. DOCKER-USER is consulted for traffic
# ROUTED to a published container port; a rule there would be visibly present
# and would filter nothing. stack/rustdesk/rustdesk-isolation.sh carries the
# long version.
#
# These ports are NEVER forwarded at the router. The remote path is the tunnel,
# which is affordable precisely because it needs no forward.
RUSTDESK_LAN_CIDR=192.168.1.0/24

# The tunnel's ranges. The v6 half is not optional: a v4-only ruleset leaves the
# same listener reachable over IPv6, and the tunnel makes a routable v6 address
# more likely rather than less. With no v6 range configured the installer still
# programs the v6 REJECT, so the listeners are closed rather than open by
# omission.
RUSTDESK_TUNNEL_CIDR=100.64.0.0/10
RUSTDESK_TUNNEL_CIDR6=fd7a:115c:a1e0::/48

# Pinned concrete tag, like every other image here. 1.1.16 is the current
# stable release. Both hbbs and hbbr run from this ONE image, differing only in
# the command they are given.
RUSTDESK_IMAGE_TAG=1.1.16

# The relay address hbbs hands to clients that cannot connect directly. Without
# it a client with no direct path is told nothing to fall back to. Use the hub's
# tunnel address so it is correct from away as well as on the LAN.
RUSTDESK_RELAY_HOST=

RUSTDESK_TCP_PORTS=21115,21116,21117,21118,21119
RUSTDESK_UDP_PORTS=21116

# ── Hub recovery: watchdog, early kill, loop brake (SR-049..SR-051) ──────────
#
# OFF BY DEFAULT, AND "ON" IS A PROMISE OF EFFORT, NOT A GUARANTEE. When enabled
# and capable, the hub recovers on its own from a memory thrash or a wedge: it
# kills the offending container, then reboots GRACEFULLY, and only when software
# can no longer act does the hardware watchdog reset the board. Every reboot is
# counted, and three counted reboots in 24 h engage a BRAKE that stops the box
# rebooting itself (and sets kernel.panic=0, so a panic stays down rather than
# looping). stack/recovery/README.md says what it does and - as important -
# what it does NOT do.
#
# NOT A COMPOSE SERVICE: host units installed by firstboot step 6g and
# stack/recovery/setup-recovery.sh. What is ALWAYS on, whatever this says, is
# record-only: the 30 s `hub-pressure` sample, boot accounting, a pinned
# persistent journal, and kernel.hung_task_warnings=-1.
#
# Turning it OFF again is `HUB_WATCHDOG_ENABLED=false` and re-running
# setup-recovery.sh (firstboot does that every boot): it DISARMS the hardware
# timer first, then removes everything the "on" state installed. Never stop the
# supervisor with a bare `systemctl stop` - use `sudo homehub-recovery disarm`.
HUB_WATCHDOG_ENABLED=false

# What "on" means, staged as the design's rollout:
#   contain  kill the stalled container, restart sshd, quarantine a repeat
#            victim; reboots are only RECORDED (P3)
#   full     also graceful reboot (L3), the hardware watchdog (L4) and
#            kernel.panic=10 (L5) (P4 - gated on lab and on-hub expiry tests)
HUB_WD_TIERS=contain

# PSI memory `full avg10` percent that counts as a pressured sample. 40 is a
# starting point owned by the build (Owner D3), to be RECALIBRATED from a week
# of hub-pressure samples before L1/L3 are enabled. It is not promised to have
# caught 2026-10-04: no PSI was recorded that night.
HUB_WD_PSI_FULL=40
# A container is killed after an episode lasts this long AND at least this
# percent of the window's MEASURED samples were pressured. Scheduling gaps are
# never counted as pressure.
HUB_WD_KILL_SECONDS=120
HUB_WD_KILL_MEASURED_PCT=75
# Graceful reboot when an episode is still open this long after it began
# (kills do not restart the clock). Must exceed HUB_WD_KILL_SECONDS.
HUB_WD_REBOOT_SECONDS=600
# Consecutive failed sshd banner probes, 60 s apart and only while memory is
# normal, before `systemctl restart ssh`.
HUB_WD_SSH_FAILS=3
# Counted reboots (recovery, watchdog, panic, and unexplained until the board
# proves it reports watchdog resets) in 24 h that engage the brake.
HUB_WD_MAX_RECOVERY_BOOTS=3
# Containers (by container name or compose service) the early kill never picks
# (Owner D9): the front door and DNS. They keep their own memory caps, and every
# hub-pressure sample logs them in full so a pass never becomes a blind spot.
HUB_WD_PROTECT=caddy,technitium
# Hardware watchdog timeout while the supervisor runs, and the timeout it hands
# to the shutdown (also systemd's RebootWatchdogSec) on every reboot.
HUB_WD_HW_TIMEOUT=60
HUB_WD_SHUTDOWN_SECONDS=300
# The reset device for the full tier: auto tries wdat_wdt (this hub has an ACPI
# WDAT table), then iTCO_wdt, then softdog. Or pin one, or `none`. Reset on
# expiry is UNPROVEN on this board until the planned test with someone at home.
HUB_WD_RESET_DEVICE=auto
# earlyoom's fast-spike backstop (installed only when enabled): SIGTERM when
# BOTH available memory and free swap fall below the first percent of each
# pair, SIGKILL below the second.
HUB_OOM_MEM_PCT=5,2
HUB_OOM_SWAP_PCT=10,5

# ── Meeting recordings from the wall panel (WSN-097; SR-110, SR-111) ────────
# The hub collects the panel's finished meeting recordings over ssh (a key of
# its own, which the panel's wall-meeting account accepts for one forced
# command) onto /srv/scratch/meetings, the scratch volume's 2 GiB `meetings`
# project, and ages them out. Not backed up, never shared (storage-map 3c).
# OFF unless true. stack/meetings/README.md has the procedure, including the
# panel's half (WALL_MEETING_PULL_PUBKEY in its wall.env).
MEETING_PULL_ENABLED=false
# The panel's address; empty = PANEL_IP above. The account is the panel's.
MEETING_PANEL_HOST=
MEETING_PANEL_USER=wall-meeting
# Recordings older than this many days are deleted (the Owner may change it:
# 2 GiB at ~21.6 MB an hour is ~99 hours, ~89 under the 90 % high-water, so 14
# days fits ~6 hours of meetings a day), and then the oldest while meetings/ is
# above this percentage of its quota.
MEETING_RETENTION_DAYS=14
MEETING_QUOTA_HIGH_PCT=90

# ── Hub continuous monitoring (SN-048, SR-085..SR-089) ───────────────────────
#
# RECORD AND ALERT ONLY: nothing here acts on the box. Host timers installed by
# firstboot step 6h from stack/monitoring/ (README there). Alerts go to ntfy on
# HOMEHUB_ALERT_TOPIC through stack/monitoring/homehub-alert.sh, rate-limited per
# key; when ntfy is down the journal line is the record. An UNSET knob below
# means its code default, which for the four switches is ON. (T0.)
#
# A DRILL never edits this file: put the drill knob in
# /etc/homehub-monitor/override.env (read after this file, by the monitors
# only), run the unit, then delete the override. stack/monitoring/README.md.
HOMEHUB_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=true
HOMEHUB_DISK_CHECK_ENABLED=true
HOMEHUB_WATCH_ENABLED=true
HOMEHUB_LIVENESS_ENABLED=true
# Seconds before the same alert key may be delivered again.
HOMEHUB_ALERT_REPEAT_SEC=1800
# Telemetry alarms (every 30 s sample): the hottest CPU sensor / the NVMe above
# its limit for HOMEHUB_THERMAL_SAMPLES samples (clears 5 C below); the kernel's
# throttle counters rising (clears after the quiet count); every core below the
# floor while at least the busy percent, for HOMEHUB_FREQ_SAMPLES samples.
HOMEHUB_THERMAL_CPU_MAX_C=90
HOMEHUB_THERMAL_NVME_MAX_C=75
HOMEHUB_THERMAL_SAMPLES=4
HOMEHUB_THROTTLE_CLEAR_SAMPLES=10
HOMEHUB_FREQ_FLOOR_MHZ=800
HOMEHUB_FREQ_BUSY_PCT=75
HOMEHUB_FREQ_SAMPLES=6
# Low-disk alarm: NVMe mount points only (a USB drive is never statfs'd). Each
# entry PATH, PATH=N% or PATH=NG; without a floor, the smaller of 10 % and 5 GB.
# Add /srv/scratch here once the scratch volume exists (HOMEHUB_SCRATCH_ENABLED):
# HOMEHUB_DISK_MOUNTS="/ /var/lib/docker /boot /srv/scratch"
HOMEHUB_DISK_MOUNTS="/ /var/lib/docker /boot"
# Watches (stack/monitoring/watch.table): row names to switch off, space-separated.
HOMEHUB_WATCH_DISABLE=
# The drill-only synthetic watch: a process comm that must be present, and the
# minutes it may be absent. Empty = no synthetic watch. Set it in the override
# file, never here.
HOMEHUB_WATCH_SYNTHETIC_PROCESS=
HOMEHUB_WATCH_SYNTHETIC_ABSENT_MIN=10
# Plan W3b watches, each on only with its component's own switch: the door
# recorder's newest segment (DOOR_RECORDER_ENABLED; minutes since the newest
# /srv/scratch/door/*/door-*.ts* was written, the open .ts.part included), the
# scratch volume's identity per homehub_scratch.py status (HOMEHUB_SCRATCH_ENABLED;
# minutes of consecutive bad runs), and the meeting pull (MEETING_PULL_ENABLED;
# minutes without a successful run of homehub-meeting-pull.service). 0 = fire at
# the next run: a drill value, set in the override file, never here.
HOMEHUB_WATCH_DOOR_FRESH_MIN=15
HOMEHUB_WATCH_SCRATCH_MIN=5
HOMEHUB_WATCH_MEETING_PULL_MIN=30
# The scratch status tool the scratch-mount watch runs. Unset = the deployed
# /opt/homehub/stack/scratch/homehub_scratch.py; a drill points it at a path
# that does not exist (the watch must alert on a status that cannot run).
# HOMEHUB_WATCH_SCRATCH_TOOL=
# Liveness probe (every 60 s). Empty = the default target: DNS from LAN_IP for
# <TRACKER_SUBDOMAIN>.<DOMAIN>, HTTPS from Caddy on 127.0.0.1:443 with that SNI.
HOMEHUB_LIVENESS_DNS_SERVER=
HOMEHUB_LIVENESS_DNS_NAME=
HOMEHUB_LIVENESS_HTTPS_HOST=
HOMEHUB_LIVENESS_HTTPS_PORT=443
HOMEHUB_LIVENESS_HTTPS_NAME=
# Consecutive failed runs before the one evidence bundle and the alert.
HOMEHUB_LIVENESS_FAILURES=3
# The evidence bundle's journal extract is limited to these units and syslog
# tags (space-separated). Unset = the defaults in homehub_evidence.py.
# HOMEHUB_EVIDENCE_JOURNAL_UNITS="docker.service containerd.service ..."
# HOMEHUB_EVIDENCE_JOURNAL_TAGS="kernel hub-pressure homehub-recovery-boot sshd systemd-journald"

# ── NVMe scratch volume (SN-050, SR-102..SR-104; stack/scratch/README.md) ─────
# An 18 GiB LV `hub-scratch` in the root disk's VG, ext4 with project quotas,
# mounted at /srv/scratch (nofail; the bare mount point underneath is mode 000).
# One directory per writer, each a quota project with a HARD limit: door/
# (the door recorder), meetings/ (W6), ottertube/ (W8). Never backed up, never
# shared. `true` makes firstboot create it when absent (after the docker LV) and
# re-assert it on every boot; creation STOPS, changing nothing, when the VG
# would keep less than HOMEHUB_SCRATCH_VG_RESERVE_MIB unallocated.
HOMEHUB_SCRATCH_ENABLED=false
# The filesystem's UUID, recorded when it is first made (`homehub_scratch.py
# ensure` prints it). Every writer's guard compares the mounted device with it,
# and a reimage recreates the filesystem WITH it, so the writers keep working.
# Empty = not created yet: every writer refuses to start.
HOMEHUB_SCRATCH_UUID=
HOMEHUB_SCRATCH_SIZE_MIB=18432
HOMEHUB_SCRATCH_VG_RESERVE_MIB=4096
# Usable space that stays free with every writer at its quota. The quotas are
# sized after mkfs from the filesystem's real usable space; ottertube is reduced
# first, then meetings, if they do not fit.
HOMEHUB_SCRATCH_FREE_FLOOR_MIB=2048
HOMEHUB_SCRATCH_MIN_QUOTA_MIB=512
HOMEHUB_SCRATCH_QUOTA_DOOR_MIB=8192
HOMEHUB_SCRATCH_QUOTA_MEETINGS_MIB=2048
HOMEHUB_SCRATCH_QUOTA_OTTERTUBE_MIB=6144

# ── Door relay and recorder (SN-050, SR-105..SR-107; stack/door/README.md) ────
# The doorbell accepts ONE video connection. The relay holds it and re-serves it
# at rtsp://<LAN_IP>:<DOOR_RELAY_RTSP_PORT>/<DOOR_RELAY_STREAM> for the panel's
# door view and the recorder, with the doorbell's own credential (no new
# secret). LAN-only: no Caddy route, no DNS name. Its lifecycle belongs to
# homehub-door-relay.service: NEVER put `door-relay` in COMPOSE_PROFILES.
DOOR_RELAY_ENABLED=false
# IMAGE:VERSION@DIGEST - must equal stack/door/door-relay-image.pin.
DOOR_RELAY_IMAGE=alexxit/go2rtc:1.9.14@sha256:675c318b23c06fd862a61d262240c9a63436b4050d177ffc68a32710d9e05bae
DOOR_RELAY_RTSP_PORT=8554
DOOR_RELAY_API_PORT=1984
DOOR_RELAY_STREAM=door
# The camera, as the relay reaches it. Deliberately NOT the panel's
# DOORBELL_RTSP_* names: after the cutover the panel's DOORBELL_RTSP_HOST is the
# RELAY, and one name holding two facts on two machines is how a reimage points
# the relay at itself. The host is private (T1, HomeHub config); the password is
# the doorbell's own store key (T3), the same value the panel uses.
DOOR_RELAY_UPSTREAM_HOST=
DOOR_RELAY_UPSTREAM_PORT=554
DOOR_RELAY_UPSTREAM_PATH=/H.264
DOOR_RELAY_UPSTREAM_USERNAME=admin
DOOR_RELAY_UPSTREAM_PASSWORD=
# What `door_relay.py probe` and `gate` require the relay to deliver: the
# camera's own stream, unchanged (an ffprobe codec name; WIDTHxHEIGHT).
DOOR_RELAY_EXPECT_CODEC=h264
DOOR_RELAY_EXPECT_SIZE=1536x1536
# The recorder: copy-only, video-only segments in /srv/scratch/door/YYYY-MM-DD/
# (UTC), cut at the first keyframe after each DOOR_RECORDER_SEGMENT_SEC; whole
# oldest segments pruned to stay under DOOR_RECORDER_CAP_MIB (keep it under
# door's quota). Needs the scratch volume; refuses to start without it.
DOOR_RECORDER_ENABLED=false
DOOR_RECORDER_SEGMENT_SEC=600
DOOR_RECORDER_CAP_MIB=7680
DOOR_RECORDER_STALL_SEC=30

# ── OtterTube: the kids' app site, its update channel, and the curated video library (SN-051; stack/ottertube/README.md) ──
# TWO SWITCHES (SR-126), split on 2026-10-26 so the site and the update channel
# can outlive the video library.
#
# THE LIBRARY: `true` (exactly) runs the daily download of the PRIVATE curated
# list (stack/ottertube/config/list.json, from HomeHub config) onto the scratch
# volume, rebuilds OtterTube's catalogue and runs the media server behind
# /ottertube/catalog.json and /ottertube/media/. Needs the scratch volume
# (HOMEHUB_SCRATCH_ENABLED) and refuses to write without it. Its lifecycle
# belongs to the homehub-ottertube-* units: NEVER put `ottertube` in
# COMPOSE_PROFILES.
OTTERTUBE_ENABLED=false
# THE SITE AND THE UPDATE CHANNEL: `true` (exactly) serves
# https://<OTTERTUBE_SUBDOMAIN>.<DOMAIN>/ottertube/ on the LAN only - the web
# build, the signed app updates (stack/ottertube/updates, SR-125) and the
# library's catalogue and media when the library is on - and creates its DNS
# record. Anything else turns the site off (Caddy answers 404, no record).
# EMPTY FOLLOWS OTTERTUBE_ENABLED: what every .env written before the split
# has, so a hub that ran the library keeps its site. Set it explicitly to keep
# the site when the library goes.
OTTERTUBE_SITE_ENABLED=
# The site's label: a Technitium pinpoint record (no wildcard, no public record)
# is created for it only while the site knob is on.
OTTERTUBE_SUBDOMAIN=ottertube
# IMAGE:VERSION@DIGEST - must equal stack/ottertube/ottertube-images.pin.
OTTERTUBE_YTDL_SUB_IMAGE=ghcr.io/jmbannon/ytdl-sub:2026.08.26.post1@sha256:f96bcf1d2896da0177f9c7964407c27830571d1eb96a5886abd605140c69e278
OTTERTUBE_INDEXER_IMAGE=node:24.22.0-alpine3.24@sha256:44fce696f38ba8c987c4db89954051000bbd05ffd8d8659ba15ed4a8602e937f
# FINITE BY CONSTRUCTION. Hosts a list item may come from (each also needs a
# single-item rule in ottertube.py HOST_RULES); add one only after its terms
# were reviewed. The list's size estimates must total at most BUDGET (well
# inside the 6 GiB ottertube quota, which also holds a download in flight);
# yt-dlp skips any single file over MAX_FILESIZE.
OTTERTUBE_ALLOWED_HOSTS="images-assets.nasa.gov video.blender.org"
OTTERTUBE_BUDGET_MIB=3072
OTTERTUBE_MAX_ITEMS=40
OTTERTUBE_MAX_FILESIZE_MIB=400
OTTERTUBE_ITEM_TIMEOUT_SEC=1800
# Seconds between two downloads (randomised 0.5x-1.5x): polite to the sources.
OTTERTUBE_PAUSE_SEC=20
# The indexer's grace before a media file the catalogue dropped is unpublished.
OTTERTUBE_GRACE_DAYS=7
